WEBVTT

00:00:00.790 --> 00:00:34.760
<v Ira Glass>Immigration officials have been doing a lot of things lately that seem very hard to explain-- ignoring court orders, shooting civilians. And when asked why these things are happening, as we've all seen, we don't exactly get a frank and honest account from officials. What we get are talking points, many of which don't turn out to be true. But recently, refreshingly, two different lawyers who work for ICE stepped forward separately and lifted the curtain to reveal all kinds of details about what is really happening right now inside our immigration system.
<v Ira Glass>移民官员最近一直在做很多很难解释的事情——忽视法院命令、向平民开枪。正如我们大家所见，当被问及这些事情为什么会发生时，我们并没有从官员那里得到坦率诚实的说法。我们得到的都是说辞，其中许多最后都被证明并非事实。但最近，令人耳目一新的是，两名在移民及海关执法局（ICE）工作的不同律师分别站了出来，拉开帷幕，揭露了当前我们移民系统内部正在发生各种真实情况。

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:56.200
<v Ira Glass>And I know there have been a couple of quotes from each of these people in the news. But I have to say, when you dive into the details of everything that they witnessed, it's satisfying in this way that I really didn't even know I craved. Because they're not talking about, like, this or that specific incident, but about the entire system being broken. And they lay it out.
<v Ira Glass>我知道新闻里有这两位各自的一些引言。但我必须说，当你深入了解他们目睹的一切细节时，你会有一种我甚至不知道自己以前曾渴望过的满足感。因为他们谈论的不是这起或那起具体的事件，而是整个系统的崩溃。而且他们把这一切都摆在了台面上。

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:06.990
<v Ira Glass>Today on our show, what we're going to do is we're going to bring you their stories, and I think you'll see what I mean. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. And we're just going to jump right in right now with Act One.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，我们要为大家带来他们的故事，我想你会明白我的意思。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《这个美国生活》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。我们现在就直接切入第一幕。

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:23.830
<v Ira Glass>Act One, I got the memo. OK. So this thing just happened two weeks ago, that this lawyer from ICE walked into a room full of congresspeople and gave testimony that basically just blew up his entire life, like everything he'd worked towards for years.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，我收到了备忘录。好的。事情就在两周前发生，这位来自ICE的律师走进了一个坐满国会议员的房间，作出了基本上彻底毁了他整个人生的证词——就像毁了他多年来为之努力的一切一样。

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:34.750
<v Ira Glass>What was so extreme that he decided to stop training ICE agents and stop lawyering for ICE agents and instead became a whistleblower? Our producer, Nadia Reiman, talked to him.
<v Ira Glass>究竟是什么事情如此极端，以至于他决定停止培训ICE特工、不再为ICE特工担任法律顾问，转而成为一名举报人？我们的制作人娜迪亚·雷曼（Nadia Reiman）采访了他。

00:01:34.750 --> 00:02:02.620
<v Nadia Reiman>This whistleblower, Ryan Schwank, will tell you proudly that he's a public servant. Doesn't make policy and doesn't want to, thanks very much. Not that he always agrees, but he's fine executing whatever the people in charge want to do, as long as it's legal. He started working for the government under President Biden. He'd been representing immigrants, but now he was representing ICE in the Department of Homeland Security, prosecuting immigration cases.
<v Nadia Reiman>这位举报人瑞安·施万克（Ryan Schwank）会自豪地告诉你，他是一名公务员。他不做政策，也不想做，谢谢。倒不是说他总是赞同，但只要是合法的，他很乐意执行掌权者想做的任何事。他在拜登总统任内开始为政府工作。他以前一直在代表移民，但现在他在国土安全部代表ICE，负责起诉移民案件。

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:23.980
<v Nadia Reiman>The way he saw it, he was fulfilling the same purpose in both roles-- ensuring procedural fairness, due process, making sure the system worked the same for everyone and worked smoothly. It bugged him if things got mucky or slow, like when the Biden administration was dragging its feet on immigration cases.
<v Nadia Reiman>在他看来，他在两个角色中履行的是相同的职责——确保程序公平、正义，确保系统对每个人都公平运转且运作顺畅。如果事情变得混乱或缓慢，比如拜登政府在移民案件上拖拖拉拉，他会觉得很烦恼。

00:02:23.980 --> 00:03:01.360
<v Nadia Reiman>But then last May, under the Trump administration, all ICE lawyers got instructions that Ryan found alarming. They were told to ask for their cases to be dismissed when they went to court, which allowed ICE agents to arrest immigrants right there and then, often as soon as they walked out the door. Ryan was like, how could people have due process if there's no time to make their case and then they're getting immediately arrested? He wasn't sure what to do, but he's such a rule follower that he didn't tell anyone about these new instructions, not even his wife.
<v Nadia Reiman>但就在去年5月，在特朗普政府领导下，所有ICE律师都接到了瑞安觉得令人担忧的指示。他们被告知在出庭时要求撤销案件，这使得ICE特工可以当场逮捕移民，通常就在他们走出大门的那一刻。瑞安觉得，如果没有时间陈述案情就被立即逮捕，人们怎么能获得正义？他不知道该怎么办，但他是个极其循规蹈矩的人，所以他没有把这些新指示告诉任何人，甚至没有告诉他的妻子。

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.340
<v Ryan Schwank>That's kind of the problem with being a lawyer, right? I can talk to her about generalities. I can't talk to her about specific policies.
<v Ryan Schwank>作为一名律师，这大概就是问题所在吧？我可以和她谈论一般情况。我不能和她谈论具体的政策。

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:09.360
<v Nadia Reiman>Oh. So you were just like, I'm generally feeling this?
<v Nadia Reiman>哦。所以你当时就像是，我只是大概有这种感觉？

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:22.270
<v Ryan Schwank>Kind of, yeah. I try to be very careful with what I would say to not give away agency practices. But I explained to her there's kind of thing is happening. And then, of course, it was out in the news, like, two days later, right?
<v Ryan Schwank>差不多，是的。我尽量对自己的言辞非常小心，以免泄露机构的做法。但我向她解释说，某种事情正在发生。然后，当然，大约两天后这就在新闻里曝光了，对吧？

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:25.960
<v Nadia Reiman>Yeah. Did she advise you to do something? Or was she like, here's what I think you--
<v Nadia Reiman>是的。她有没有建议你做点什么？或者她当时是不是说，我觉得你应该——

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:36.360
<v Ryan Schwank>She just said follow your ethical guidelines. She was the one actually, I think, suggested calling the bar association. So that's what I did. She's like, you should call that hotline. I know they have one.
<v Ryan Schwank>她只是说遵循你的道德准则。实际上，我认为正是她建议打电话给律师协会的。所以我照做了。她说，你应该打那个热线。我知道他们有一个。

00:03:36.360 --> 00:04:08.310
<v Nadia Reiman>So he did. He called the state bar association. And they said, yep, that's not ethical. He talked to his boss. His boss said, OK, I get it. You don't have to do these. I'll do them. But Ryan figured that he could only dodge these for so long. So he started to wonder what was next for him. Then one day his supervisor told him, you know, with this surge of thousands of new ICE agents DHS is hiring, they need people to train them in Glynco, Georgia. You interested?
<v Nadia Reiman>于是他照办了。他给州律师协会打了电话。他们说，没错，这不道德。他跟老板谈了谈。他的老板说，好吧，我明白了。你不用做这些。我来做。但瑞安琢磨着，他躲得过初一躲不过十五。于是他开始纳闷自己接下来该怎么办。后来有一天，上司告诉他，你知道的，随着DHS（国土安全部）正在招募数千名新ICE特工，他们需要人去佐治亚州格林科（Glynco）培训他们。你有兴趣吗？

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:23.210
<v Ryan Schwank>And to me, it seemed like a great opportunity. One, part of it was self-interested. Like, if I'm the attorney who's in Glynco, teaching, I'm not the attorney in the courtroom who's having to answer the ethical question of, am I OK prosecuting this case?
<v Ryan Schwank>对我来说，这似乎是一个绝佳的机会。第一，其中有一部分是出于自私的考量。比如，如果我是去格林科教书的律师，我就不是法庭上那个必须回答道德问题的律师，去纠结“我起诉这个案件真的没问题吗？”。

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:27.290
<v Nadia Reiman>You were just like, not it, I'm going to dodge and go do this other thing so that I don't get--
<v Nadia Reiman>你当时的想法就是，不是我，我要开溜去做别的事，这样我就不会——

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:59.380
<v Ryan Schwank>I would love to say otherwise. I would love to be like, this was just 100% profile-in-courage type stuff. But it's not. It's a combination of I could see the writing on the wall and I saw the opportunity to do good. This was an opportunity to go teach, which is something I've always liked doing. But then, of course, I got to Glynco, and all of that changed the day I got there.
<v Ryan Schwank>我很想说点别的。我很想标榜说这纯粹是百分之百彰显勇气的行为。但事实并非如此。这既是因为我看到了大势所趋，也是因为我看到了行善的机会。这是一个去教书的机会，而教书一直是我喜欢做的事。但当然，我一到格林科，我到那儿的第一天这一切就都变了。

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:19.220
<v Ryan Schwank>So September 1 was the day I arrived in Glynco, Georgia. I have to go back and check my records, but I think September 2 was the first morning that I went to the academy. I showed up early in the morning. And I had to go through and get my badge. And then I went to the academy training building.
<v Ryan Schwank>所以9月1日是我到达佐治亚州格林科的日子。我得回去查查记录，但我认为9月2日是我去学院的第一个早晨。我一大早就去了。我得进去拿到我的徽章。然后我去了学院的培训大楼。

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:55.490
<v Ryan Schwank>And while I was there, the very first thing I heard was a conversation about background checks on cadets and how they weren't being completed before they came to the academy and that we already had had problems with cadets showing up that had disqualifying criminal offenses and the concerns that we were going to have cadets coming through that we couldn't background check sufficiently to know whether or not they were going to be able to finish the program or be allowed to carry a firearm.
<v Ryan Schwank>当我在那里的时候，我听到第一件事就是关于对学员进行背景调查的对话，以及为什么这些他们在来学院之前没有完成，并且我们已经遇到了有取消资格的前科的学员来报到的问题，还有我们接下来将面临的担忧——即会有一些我们无法进行充分背景调查的学员通过审核，从而无法得知他们是否能够完成项目或被允许携带枪支。

00:05:55.490 --> 00:06:13.370
<v Ryan Schwank>You took an agency that hires maybe a thousand people a year and told it to process 10,000 people in three months. There simply weren't the resources to run all the background checks ahead of time. They were just delayed. They were backlogged. They had people showing up to the academy who had not completed background checks.
<v Ryan Schwank>你把一个每年大概招收一千人的机构，命令它在三个月内处理1,0000人。根本没有足够的资源提前完成所有的背景调查。它们只是被延误了。积压如山。他们有来学院报到的人根本没有完成背景调查。

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:37.870
<v Ryan Schwank>And my first response to hearing this thing about the background checks was, wow, that's a growth like a teething pain. Like, this is a problem. This is something they're going to solve very quickly. This was a mistake because we just started. We just started this big process. This was clearly an administrative error. And they're going to fix it. As we later found out, it was an issue that recurred repeatedly at the academy.
<v Ryan Schwank>我听到背景调查这件事时的第一反应是，哇，这是成长中的阵痛。就像，这是一个问题。这是他们会很快解决的问题。这是一个失误，因为我们才刚开始。我们刚刚启动了这个宏大的流程。这显然是一个行政错误。他们会修复它的。正如我们后来发现的那样，这是一个在学院里一再重演的问题。

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:42.570
<v Nadia Reiman>And there was another issue that came up on that first day.
<v Nadia Reiman>而那天还出现了另一个问题。

00:06:42.570 --> 00:07:05.600
<v Ryan Schwank>We were in this big conference room. And my supervisor and I are in there. I think there are a couple other people in the room. I couldn't name who exactly. But she shows me the memos. My supervisor shows me these memos. And I'm told you can read it, but you're not allowed to keep a copy of it, and you're not allowed to take notes on it.
<v Ryan Schwank>我们在那个大的会议室里。我和我的上司都在那儿。我想房间里还有另外几个人。我具体说不出名字。但她向我展示了备忘录。我的上司向我展示了这些备忘录，然后我被告知你可以阅读，但不准保留副本，也不准做笔记。

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:07.840
<v Nadia Reiman>What did you make of that?
<v Nadia Reiman>你对此作何感想？

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:22.320
<v Ryan Schwank>I had never received an order like that ever in my career at any time. It was bizarre to me, and it was a massive red flag from the moment she had said that to me-- before I even read the memo, to me, was a massive red flag before I read the first word on the page.
<v Ryan Schwank>在我的职业生涯中，无论何时，我从未收到过这样的命令。这对我来说太离谱了，从她对我说的那一刻起——甚至在我阅读备忘录之前，在我看到纸上的第一个字之前，对我来说就是一个巨大的危险信号。

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.040
<v Nadia Reiman>Then he read the memo.
<v Nadia Reiman>然后他读了这份备忘录。

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:37.540
<v Ryan Schwank>It was issued by Todd Lyons, the acting director for ICE, that instructed ICE officers that they could enter into a person's home without a judicial warrant.
<v Ryan Schwank>它由ICE代理局长托德·莱昂斯（Todd Lyons）发布，指示ICE官员在没有司法搜查令的情况下可以进入个人住宅。

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:49.180
<v Nadia Reiman>In other words, instead of ICE agents getting permission from a judge to go into someone's home, they would only need to get approval from ICE using a form called I-205.
<v Nadia Reiman>换句话说，ICE特工无需获得法官的许可即可进入某人家中，他们只需要通过一份名为I-205的表格获得ICE的批准即可。

00:07:49.180 --> 00:08:27.180
<v Ryan Schwank>Well, it's funny. When I first read it, it was so innocuous sounding that at first, I didn't think there was anything wrong with it. I read through it and was like, oh, OK, so we're going to use I-205s to go into houses. OK. And then it was kind of like when you see a cartoon character, like their feet stop, and they try to brake, and they kind of, like, skid. That's kind of what my brain did as I read this. I was like, wait a second. And then I read it through again more carefully the second time. And I was like, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa. Heck.
<v Ryan Schwank>嗯，这很有趣。我第一次读到它时，它听起来是那么无害，以至于一开始我并不觉得有什么不对劲。我读了一遍，心想，哦，好吧，所以我们要用I-205表格进屋了。好吧。然后那种感觉就像你看到一个卡通人物，他们的脚停下来，试图刹车，然后滑行了一段。当我读到这里时，我的大脑差不多就是那种反应。我心想，等一下。然后我第二次更仔细地读了一遍。我心想，哇，哇，哇，哇，哇。见鬼。

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:42.740
<v Nadia Reiman>The Fourth Amendment of the US Constitution says a warrant is needed to enter someone's home. And courts have consistently said that this applies to everyone who is physically in the country, not just citizens. So Ryan saw this new policy and he thought--
<v Nadia Reiman>美国宪法第四修正案规定，进入某人家中需要搜查令。法院一再表示，这适用于身处该国的每一个人，而不仅仅是公民。所以瑞安看到这项新政策时，他想——

00:08:42.740 --> 00:09:18.530
<v Ryan Schwank>Oh, no. Oh, no. I was by no means at the time an expert on the Fourth Amendment. Obviously, I know the Fourth Amendment because I'm an attorney. But it's not like I'm a constitutional scholar on the thing, looking for things to nitpick. I'm a lawyer who knows the Fourth Amendment the way most lawyers know the Fourth Amendment-- as a thing that you learned about and apply in generalities. And even at that level, looking at this, I was like, this, this is wrong. And my instructor, my supervisor, was clear, if you're not comfortable teaching this, just tell us.
<v Ryan Schwank>哦，不。哦，不。我当时绝不是第四修正案的专家。显然我知道第四修正案，因为我是个律师。但我也不是那种专门挑刺的宪法学者。我只是一个像大多数律师那样了解第四修正案的律师——把它当成一个你了解并笼统应用的概念。即便在这个层面上，看到这个，我也觉得，这个，这是不对的。而我的教官、我的上司明确表示，如果你教这个觉得不舒服，尽管告诉我们。

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:36.370
<v Nadia Reiman>Ryan says his boss also casually brought up that the person who worked here before him had expressed concerns about it, said they felt strongly enough about these concerns to resign, and now they don't work here anymore. Like saying, nice job you got there-- would be a shame if something happened to it.
<v Nadia Reiman>瑞安说他的老板还漫不经心地提到，在他之前做这个工作的人曾对此表示过担忧，说他们对这些担忧感到强烈到足以辞职，现在他们不干了。这就好像在说，你现在的工作真不错——如果出了什么事就太可惜了。

00:09:36.370 --> 00:10:00.460
<v Ryan Schwank>I don't think I was told the story by accident. I was told all this before I was shown the memo. So it was very clear to me that I was being told all of this and then shown the memos that I understood I either teach this or I lose my job. You're going out the door, and you're going home-- and not home to work back in Indianapolis. You're going home as in you're done. You're done with the agency.
<v Ryan Schwank>我不认为上司是无意中跟我讲这个故事的。在把备忘录拿给我看之前，这些都跟我说了。所以我很清楚，他们跟我说这一切，然后让我看备忘录，意思就是我要么教这个，要么丢饭碗。你得卷铺盖走人，回家去——不是回印第安纳州的工作岗位。你的回家是指你完蛋了。你在这个机构彻底完工了。

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:03.340
<v Nadia Reiman>Ryan mumbled something about having to think it over.
<v Nadia Reiman>瑞安含糊其辞地说他需要考虑一下。

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:22.220
<v Ryan Schwank>I kind of backpedaled a little bit and kept my answer vague. Weirdly enough, my supervisor's like, do you want to go get lunch? And then we come back. And then I excused myself, and I went home. And I called my wife. And I was like, honey, I think I've kind of walked into a bear trap here.
<v Ryan Schwank>我有点退缩了，回答得很含糊。诡异的是，我上司说，你想去吃午饭吗？然后我们回来了。接着我找了个借口溜了，我回家了。我打电话给妻子，我说，亲人啊，我觉得我好像一脚踩进捕熊陷阱里了。

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:42.290
<v Nadia Reiman>And within this bear trap, another bear trap. Ryan had also been told, when you teach this, don't write anything down. Deliver the information verbally, no paper trail. So he checked the existing curriculum to see what it said about using warrants to enter people's homes.
<v Nadia Reiman>而在这个捕熊陷阱里，还有另一个捕熊陷阱。瑞安还被告知，当你教这个时，不要写下任何东西。口头传达信息，不留纸质记录。于是他查阅了现有的课程大纲，看看上面关于使用搜查令进入人们家中是怎么说的。

00:10:42.290 --> 00:11:19.550
<v Ryan Schwank>And just remember, there are written training materials for the classes. And in the training materials, it says an I-205 admin warrant is not a search warrant. You may not use it to enter into a protected or private area. So the training documents say the exact opposite of what the policy says. And I was told that we were not going to change that, that we were not to put it in writing that we trained them differently. And the whole time, I was going, I probably need to report this. I probably need to report this to somebody.
<v Ryan Schwank>请记住，课程是有书面培训材料的。在培训材料中，它写明I-205行政搜查令不是搜查令。你不得用它进入受保护或私人区域。所以培训文件的说法与政策的说法完全相反。而我被告知，我们不会改变这一点，我们绝不能以书面形式记录我们教的内容有所不同。而在这整个过程中，我一直在想，我可能需要举报这件事。我可能需要向某人举报这件事。

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:51.590
<v Ryan Schwank>So for the first couple of days, I'm buying time as I try to figure out what I want to do. I'm just kind of staying in the background, trying to stay under the radar. I start looking into our internal avenues for reporting things. And what I found was that they had all either been compromised or destroyed. Like, they had all been fired or reassigned. Civil rights, civil liberties was essentially gone. What's called the Office of the Ombudsman was essentially gone. The only thing that was left was the Office of the Inspector General for ICE.
<v Ryan Schwank>所以在头几天里，我一直在争取时间，同时琢磨着我想做什么。我基本上就是留在幕后，尽量保持低调。我开始调查我们内部的举报途径。而我发现的是，它们要么已经被破坏，要么被摧毁了。比如，所有人都被解职或调职了。民权与公民自由办公室基本上已经不复存在了。所谓的监察员办公室基本上也不存在了。唯一剩下的就是ICE的督察长办公室。

00:11:51.590 --> 00:11:55.410
<v Nadia Reiman>The Office of the Inspector General, OIG.
<v Nadia Reiman>督察长办公室，简称OIG。

00:11:55.410 --> 00:12:05.490
<v Ryan Schwank>And the rumors I saw online were that if you reported to OIG, there's a good chance that OIG would out you to your own management.
<v Ryan Schwank>而我在网上看到的传言是，如果你向OIG举报，OIG很有可能会把你出卖给你的直属管理层。

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:08.810
<v Nadia Reiman>So you have nobody left to report it to, basically.
<v Nadia Reiman>所以基本上你已经找不到任何人可以举报了。

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:39.400
<v Ryan Schwank>I had one group I could report it to. And that was Congress. So I remember this. I was sitting in the hotel room I was in, in a chair, looking out the window at the gas station across the street. And I was like, I don't have a choice. I have to do this. If I don't report this, nobody is going to report this, and it's going to come out years later when people have been hurt because of it. I have to report this.
<v Ryan Schwank>我还有一个组织可以举报。那就是国会。我记得当时的情形。我坐在酒店房间的一把椅子上，看着窗外街对面的加油站。我心想，我别无选择。我必须这么做。如果我不举报，没有人会举报，几年后当有人因此受到伤害时，这件事才会曝光。我必须举报。

00:12:39.400 --> 00:13:03.940
<v Ryan Schwank>And so I went through-- there was a process for filing a whistleblower claim with the Oversight Committee at the House. Back in September, I made my initial report. I just described in very brief detail what was happening. And then a couple of days later, they reached out to me to set up a call. And I started to tell them a little bit about what was happening. But I told them, look, I can tell you what I saw, but I can't show you proof of it.
<v Ryan Schwank>于是我经历了一套流程——众议院监督委员会有一个提交举报人诉求的流程。早在9月份，我就做了最初的举报。我用非常简短的细节描述了正在发生的事情。几天后，他们联系我安排了一次通话。我开始向他们讲述一些正在发生的事情。但我告诉他们，听着，我可以告诉你我看到了什么，但我无法向你出示证据。

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:04.580
<v Nadia Reiman>Oh, man.
<v Nadia Reiman>哦，天哪。

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:06.600
<v Ryan Schwank>I don't have the memo.
<v Ryan Schwank>我没有备忘录。

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:18.640
<v Nadia Reiman>So, clearly, the House would have to vet Ryan's report, which can take weeks or months. And all that had to happen without Ryan getting outed to anybody at ICE.
<v Nadia Reiman>很显然，众议院将不得不对瑞安的报告进行审查，这可能需要数周或数月的时间。而这一切都必须在瑞安没有被ICE的任何人出卖的前提下进行。

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:23.300
<v Nadia Reiman>Why were you deciding to still stay at that point?
<v Nadia Reiman>你当时为什么决定还是留下来？

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:57.030
<v Ryan Schwank>If not me, then who? If I left, it would not change what they do. It would not change how things were done. I had had this conversation with myself and with my wife and with others. If I left, who was going to replace me? And the answer was going to be someone who had no frame of reference for the way things should look.
<v Ryan Schwank>如果不是我，那是谁？如果我离开，也不会改变他们的所作所为。也不会改变事情的处理方式。我曾与自己、妻子以及其他人进行过这样的对话。如果我走了，谁来取代我？答案将会是一个对事物本该有的样子毫无概念的人。

00:13:57.030 --> 00:14:13.070
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, while Ryan stays at his job, he witnesses all kinds of other things in the cadet training that he finds very, very worrisome as somebody who wants capable ICE agents out on the street. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，在瑞安留任期间，作为一名希望合格的ICE特工走上街头的人，他在学员培训中目睹了各种其他让他感到非常非常担忧的事情。请继续收听。

00:14:13.070 --> 00:14:19.510
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. Nadia Reiman's story about ICE whistleblower Ryan Schwank continues.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《这个美国生活》。娜迪亚·雷曼关于ICE举报人瑞安·施万克的故事仍在继续。

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:46.540
<v Nadia Reiman>So Ryan stays and teaches at Glynco, Georgia, at the ICE academy there. It's at a training campus by the beach. It has things like a mat room, literally a room full of mats to practice tackling someone down. It has a fake town, like a movie set, to practice arrests, classrooms, a cafeteria, shooting ranges. Ryan says it was strange to be right by the ocean next to towns that are vacation destinations and still hear gunshots all day long.
<v Nadia Reiman>所以瑞安留下来，在佐治亚州格林科的移民及海关执法局（ICE）学院教书。那是海滩边的一个训练营地。那里有垫子室之类的设施，字面意思就是一个铺满垫子的房间，用来练习把人扑倒制服。那里还有一个像电影布景一样的假小镇，用来练习抓捕，此外还有教室、食堂和射击场。瑞安说，身处大洋边缘、紧邻度假胜地般的城镇，却整天都能听到枪声，这种感觉非常怪异。

00:14:46.540 --> 00:15:09.860
<v Nadia Reiman>Ryan started teaching after immigration agents had spent the summer blanketing Los Angeles but before the big surges in Chicago and Minneapolis. He says his team was told that about 3,000 cadets who had no prior law enforcement experience were coming to the campus in Georgia. And his job was to help turn them into new ICE agents. DHS wanted them to graduate by Christmas.
<v Nadia Reiman>瑞安开始教学时，正值移民特工在洛杉矶度过了一个夏天的全面部署之后、但在芝加哥和明尼阿波利斯出现大规模增派之前。他说，他的团队接到通知，大约3000名没有任何执法经验的新学员将来到佐治亚州的校园。他的工作就是协助把他们培养成新一代的ICE特工。国土安全部希望他们在圣诞节前毕业。

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:32.840
<v Nadia Reiman>So Ryan is looking at the curriculum, and he's trying to figure out how to teach these cadets and teach them fast. Rules around the use of force, basic immigration law, how to react when someone's running away-- all this stuff has to be so ingrained in these cadets that they don't even think about it when they're out on the field. They just do the right thing instinctively, reach for the right tool, apply the right rule.
<v Nadia Reiman>于是瑞安看着课程大纲，琢磨着该如何教导这些学员，而且还要快速教会他们。关于使用武力的规则、基础移民法、当有人逃跑时该如何反应——所有这些内容都必须深深印在这些学员脑海里，以至于他们在实地执勤时甚至不需要思考。他们只需凭本能做出正确的事情，拿到正确的工具，适用正确的规则。

00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:40.930
<v Ryan Schwank>Do I use verbal commands? Do I use handcuffs? Do I use physical restraints? Do I use pepper spray? Do I use a taser? Do I use a baton? Do I use a gun?
<v Ryan Schwank>我该使用口头指令吗？我该使用手铐吗？我该使用身体约束吗？我该使用胡椒喷雾吗？我该使用泰瑟枪吗？我该使用警棍吗？我该使用枪支吗？

00:15:40.930 --> 00:15:46.570
<v Nadia Reiman>Were you teaching all of that in the context of, here's what you're legally allowed to do, like that was your part of the training?
<v Nadia Reiman>你教导所有这些内容时，背景是“以下是你法律上被允许做的事情”，就像那是你负责的培训部分吗？

00:15:46.570 --> 00:16:08.150
<v Ryan Schwank>That was something I was supposed to teach, but they cut it from the curriculum. There was a class the lawyers would teach that focused on the law behind use of force. And it was part of the broader scope of classes we would teach about the officer's constitutional authority and the limitations of their authority.
<v Ryan Schwank>那本该是我要教的内容的一部分，但他们把它从课程大纲里删除了。曾经有一门由律师授课的课程，重点讲解使用武力背后的法律。它是我们所教授的更广泛课程体系的一部分，涉及官员的宪法权力和权力的局限性。

00:16:08.150 --> 00:16:11.770
<v Nadia Reiman>Wow. When did you realize that that was cut?
<v Nadia Reiman>哇。你是什么时候意识到这一部分被删掉的？

00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:13.970
<v Ryan Schwank>When I saw the curriculum.
<v Ryan Schwank>当我看到课程大纲的时候。

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:55.150
<v Nadia Reiman>Ryan realized when he was comparing the new curriculum to the old one that a lot of stuff, like a comprehensive firearms exam, had been cut, while other stuff had been so compressed, he found it hard to believe it would be understandable, let alone absorbable. Like, for example, a class about the Constitution that used to be two hours and a class on the limits to use of authority that also used to be two hours-- he says they were now squeezed into one short lecture that also covered other topics, like immigration and civil liberties. And that's just one example of the ways the curriculum had been compressed. Total teaching hours had been cut by 40%.
<v Nadia Reiman>瑞安在对比新旧课程大纲时发现，很多内容——比如全面的枪械考试——被删掉了，而其他内容则被压缩得极其厉害，他甚至难以相信这些内容还能被理解，更不用说被消化了。比如，一门原本需要两小时的关于宪法的课程，以及一门同样需要两小时的关于使用权力限制的课程——他说，它们现在被挤压进了一个简短的讲座中，而该讲座还要涵盖移民和公民自由等其他主题。这只是课程被压缩的其中一个例子。总教学时间被削减了40%。

00:16:55.150 --> 00:17:01.230
<v Ryan Schwank>So have you ever seen a tree die from the inside? They rot from the inside outwards?
<v Ryan Schwank>那么，你见过树木从内部枯死吗？它们是从内部向外腐烂的？

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:01.870
<v Nadia Reiman>No.
<v Nadia Reiman>没有。

00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:22.430
<v Ryan Schwank>So maybe you'll see this sometime if you were going to the woods. You'll see a tree that looks completely solid on the outside. The bark is intact. The shell is intact. But if you push it, it will just snap. And you'll look inside, and the whole core is gone. It's just hollowed out on the inside. That's kind of how the training academy was.
<v Ryan Schwank>所以你以后去树林时可能会见到这种情况。你会看到一棵树，从外表看完全是结实的。树皮是完好的。外壳是完好的。但如果你推它一下，它就会直接断裂。然后你往里看，整个树心都没了。里面完全被掏空了。培训学院差不多就是这个样子。

00:17:22.430 --> 00:17:27.930
<v Nadia Reiman>Meanwhile, Ryan still has to figure out how to deal with this memo. Does he teach it?
<v Nadia Reiman>与此同时，瑞安仍然得琢磨怎么处理这份备忘录。他要教它吗？

00:17:27.930 --> 00:17:32.820
<v Nadia Reiman>So did you tell cadets, when you were training them, that they should go into houses without a warrant?
<v Nadia Reiman>那你训练学员时，有没有告诉他们应该在没有搜查令的情况下进入民宅？

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:33.360
<v Ryan Schwank>No.
<v Ryan Schwank>没有。

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:34.780
<v Nadia Reiman>Or did you find a way--
<v Nadia Reiman>或者你有没有找到某种方法——

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:35.280
<v Ryan Schwank>No.
<v Ryan Schwank>没有。

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:35.700
<v Nadia Reiman>How did you--
<v Nadia Reiman>你是怎么——

00:17:35.700 --> 00:18:11.800
<v Ryan Schwank>So what I would tell cadets, I would tell cadets, this policy is out there. First and foremost, if I could avoid the subject, I would. But in the classes where it would come up, I would discuss the memo like this. I would tell cadets, this policy is out there. This policy is the agency's position on it. It has not been tested in the courts. There is nothing that says you can do this. But it's the agency's position that you can. If you're going to go out and use this, you need to check with your local management, first and foremost, because that's what they told us to say. And I would tell them, if you have questions, I would say, talk to your local OPLA office first.
<v Ryan Schwank>所以我会对学员说的是，我会告诉他们，这项政策确实存在。首先也是最重要的一点，如果能避开这个话题，我就会避开。但在会涉及这个话题的课堂上，我会这样讨论这份备忘录。我会告诉学员，这项政策是存在的。这项政策是机构对它的立场。它还没有经过法院的检验。没有任何东西表明你可以这么做。但机构的立场是你可以这么做。如果你打算出去使用这个政策，首先也是最重要的一点，你需要向当地管理层核实，因为这是他们告诉我们要这么说的。我还会告诉他们，如果你们有疑问，我会说，先去和当地的OPLA办公室谈谈。

00:18:11.800 --> 00:18:18.820
<v Nadia Reiman>OPLA, Office of the Principal Legal Advisor, a.k.a. your local on-call ICE attorneys.
<v Nadia Reiman>OPLA，首席法律顾问办公室，也就是你们当地随时待命的ICE律师。

00:18:18.820 --> 00:18:31.650
<v Ryan Schwank>Because I know my colleagues in OPLA by and large are going to tell them that this is hoodoo, that it's absolutely unlawful. And I have confidence in my colleagues to say that.
<v Ryan Schwank>因为我知道我在OPLA的同事大体上会告诉他们这是胡说八道，这是绝对非法的。我对我的同事们有这种信心。

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:48.770
<v Nadia Reiman>Often, Ryan's job involved observing cadets during role-playing exercises in the movie set, fake city part of the campus. Cadets would be given loose instructions like, target is inside that bodega, go get them. And instructors would participate sometimes but mostly just watch and give notes.
<v Nadia Reiman>通常，瑞安的工作包括在校园里那个电影布景般的假城市区域的角色扮演练习中观察学员。学员会得到一些宽泛的指令，比如：目标在那家杂货店里，去把他们抓出来。教官有时会参与其中，但大多时候只是在一旁观看并做记录。

00:18:48.770 --> 00:19:14.930
<v Ryan Schwank>Sometimes scenarios would go off script. Sometimes role players would change things. So you would sometimes see scenarios that don't normally involve firearms, for instance, turn into a firearm scenario. And when this would happen, you would see the cadets do things that would jeopardize themselves and others, like pulling firearms at times that are inappropriate or pointing firearms at people they shouldn't be pointing them at, making arrests of individuals that had not committed a crime.
<v Ryan Schwank>有时情景会脱离剧本。有时角色扮演者会改变情况。所以你有时会看到原本通常不涉及枪械的情景，比如演变成枪械情景。当这种情况发生时，你会看到学员做出危及自己和他人的事情，比如在不恰当的时候拔出枪支，或者把枪指向不该指的人，逮捕并未犯罪的个人。

00:19:14.930 --> 00:19:32.000
<v Nadia Reiman>This pointed out to Ryan bad habits or instincts that they needed to teach out of the cadets, but there was so little time. And cadets started telling him that they were going to be sent to Minneapolis, which was already underway and all over the news. He kept thinking about that during the trainings.
<v Nadia Reiman>这向瑞安指出了学员们身上需要通过教学来纠正的坏习惯或本能，但时间实在是太少了。而且学员们开始告诉他，他们将被派往明尼阿波利斯，那里的行动已经在进行中，并且登上了各大新闻。他在培训期间一直想着这件事。

00:19:32.000 --> 00:20:04.800
<v Ryan Schwank>There's this scenario where they're making an arrest outside a business. And the individual's not combative or anything like that. The officers are placing them under arrest outside a business. And in this particular scenario, we had enough role players that we had a few acting as observers, a few acting as people from the public doing exactly as in Minneapolis, which is coming up with their cell phone and filming with their cell phone and yelling, hey, what are you doing? Telling the officers that they're fascists or whatever, just like they say in Minneapolis.
<v Ryan Schwank>有这样一个情景，他们正在一家企业外面实施逮捕。而个人并没有好斗或类似的行为。警员们正在企业外面实施逮捕。在这个特定的情景中，我们有足够扮演角色的演员，其中几个人扮演旁观者，几个人扮演公众，做的事情和明尼阿波利斯发生的一模一样，也就是拿着手机走过来，用手机拍摄，大喊着“嘿，你们在干什么？”，告诉警员他们是法西斯或者诸如此类的话，就像他们在明尼阿波利斯所说的一样。

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:24.110
<v Ryan Schwank>And this one cadet was on what they call a perimeter. This one role player, protester, got, I guess, too close to the officer's comfort zone. I didn't think it was close enough. I think any of the other instructors thought it was close enough. But they thought it was close enough that they told him to back up.
<v Ryan Schwank>而其中一个学员当时在他们所谓的警戒线上。这个角色扮演者、抗议者，我猜，离让警员感到舒适的区域太近了。我不认为有那么近。我认为其他任何教官都不会觉得有那么近。但他们觉得距离近到足以命令他后退。

00:20:24.110 --> 00:21:01.470
<v Ryan Schwank>And then immediately-- I don't think they gave them time to respond, like to react to the command to back up-- pulled out their pepper spray. And this kid had simulated using the pepper spray to pepper spray this role player. And it was categorically-- I don't think a single instructor there thought that it was an appropriate use. It was not appropriate. It was not justified by the circumstances. And when we asked the cadet afterwards what happened, their explanation was that this is what they were seeing in Minneapolis, that they were doing it just like they would up there.
<v Ryan Schwank>然后立刻——我不认为他们给了对方反应的时间，比如对后退指令做出反应的时间——就拔出了他们的胡椒喷雾。这个学员模拟使用了胡椒喷雾去喷射这个角色扮演者。这绝对是——我不认为那里的任何一个教官会认为那是一种恰当的使用方式。那是不恰当的。那是情势所不 justifies 的（未被情势正当化的）。事后我们问学员发生了什么时，他们的解释是，这是他们在明尼阿波利斯看到的做法，他们这样做就像他们在那边做的一样。

00:21:01.470 --> 00:21:23.370
<v Nadia Reiman>For Ryan, what he was seeing on the news, which was experienced officers being too aggressive too often, that made him more alarmed about what he was seeing during his own shortened trainings. And what he was seeing during trainings was making him more alarmed about what he was seeing on the news. He worried his cadets weren't ready. Some cadets worried too.
<v Nadia Reiman>对瑞安来说，他在新闻上看到的那些经验丰富的特工过于频繁地表现得过于具有攻击性，这让他对自己在不断缩短的培训中所看到的情况感到更加惊恐。而他在培训中所看到的情况，又让他对自己在新闻上看到的情况感到更加惊恐。他担心他的学员还没准备好。一些学员也很担心。

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:32.850
<v Nadia Reiman>Did they seem scared? Did any of them come to you and say we don't feel prepared enough? We don't feel ready?
<v Nadia Reiman>他们看起来害怕吗？有没有人来找过你们说我们感觉准备得不够？我们感觉没准备好？

00:21:32.850 --> 00:22:09.850
<v Ryan Schwank>Yes. Yes. I had cadets say that to us. I had cadets on multiple occasions say they felt unprepared for the job. I remember one cadet was talking to us about-- we were dealing with the issue of entry into a yard. And the cadet was having a lot of difficulty understanding how to apply that set of rules. We walked them through it a couple of times. And he was talking about how everything in the academy kind of goes in one ear and out the other because they're rushed from one thing to the next to try to meet that schedule.
<v Ryan Schwank>是的。是的。我有学员对我们说过这话。我有学员在多个场合表示他们对这份工作感到准备不足。我记得有一个学员在和我们交谈——当时我们正在处理进入院子的合规问题。那个学员在理解如何应用这套规则时遇到了很大的困难。我们带他梳理了好几次。他谈到学院里的所有东西基本上都是左耳进右耳出，因为他们被催促着从一件事赶到另一件事，试图赶上那个进度表。

00:22:09.850 --> 00:22:26.640
<v Nadia Reiman>Of course, fast training can still be rigorous training, as long as you test people to make sure they got it. And Ryan says that in this new compressed training, crucial real-world skills were not adequately tested, like using a gun correctly or subduing somebody in the field.
<v Nadia Reiman>当然，快速的培训仍然可以是严格的培训，只要你对人们进行测试以确保他们掌握了。而瑞安表示，在这个新的压缩培训中，至关重要的现实世界技能并没有得到充分测试，比如正确使用枪支或在实地制服某人。

00:22:26.640 --> 00:22:48.660
<v Ryan Schwank>Under the old academy structure, what existed before the surge, these practical exercises were graded. The students had to pass these exercises to graduate. If they didn't pass the exercise, if they demonstrated a lack of knowledge of the law or the tactics involved, they had to repeat the class. And if they failed twice, they were out. They did not get to graduate.
<v Ryan Schwank>在旧的学院结构下，也就是增兵之前存在的体制，这些实操练习是计入成绩的。学员必须通过这些练习才能毕业。如果他们没有通过练习，如果他们表现出对法律或相关战术缺乏了解，他们就必须重修这门课。如果他们挂科两次，就被淘汰了。他们就无法毕业。

00:22:48.660 --> 00:22:54.480
<v Nadia Reiman>Meaning they wouldn't become an ICE officer. But now things were different.
<v Nadia Reiman>这意味着他们不会成为ICE官员。但现在情况不同了。

00:22:54.480 --> 00:23:14.960
<v Ryan Schwank>So now, no matter what we saw the cadets do in these scenarios, they still graduated. We could tell a cadet up and down that they were going to get themselves killed in the field or get arrested or violate the law with what they were doing, and I hope and I believe many cadets listened to that. But it didn't matter if they didn't. They could still graduate, no matter what we saw them do.
<v Ryan Schwank>所以现在，无论我们在这些情景中看到学员做什么，他们照样毕业。我们可以对一个学员反复强调，他们这样做会在实地送命、被捕或违法，我希望并且相信很多学员听进去了。但即使他们没听进去也无所谓。无论我们看到他们做什么，他们依然可以毕业。

00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:48.030
<v Nadia Reiman>We reached out to DHS to ask about all of this. They didn't respond. They did claim, though, in a statement in February that, quote, "No training requirements have been removed." The statement says that, actually, the training has been beefed up from 8 hours a day to 12 hours a day. It also somehow claims that the hours are the same number of hours that recruits have always received. DHS says they're teaching the Fourth and Fifth Amendments in a way that is, quote, "structured and comprehensive." They called it "integral."
<v Nadia Reiman>我们联系了国土安全部询问所有这些情况。他们没有回应。不过，他们在二月份的一份声明中确实声称：“没有任何培训要求被取消。”声明称，实际上，培训强度已经从每天8小时加强到每天12小时。它还以某种方式声称，这些小时数与新兵们一直以来接受的小时数完全相同。国土安全部表示，他们正在以一种“结构化且全面”的方式教授宪法第四修正案和第五修正案。他们称其为“不可或缺的”。

00:23:48.030 --> 00:24:07.110
<v Nadia Reiman>When Ryan first made his report, it was about the memo. That was his main concern. But now, over five months of teaching, he'd seen so many things wrong with the training, he felt like that needed its own report. And he felt like somebody needed to be the face of that to make it as credible as possible.
<v Nadia Reiman>当瑞安最初写报告时，内容是关于那份备忘录的。那是他最主要的关切。但现在，经过五个月的教学，他发现培训中存在太多问题，他觉得这需要有他自己的专属报告。他觉得必须有人成为这件事的代言人，以使其尽可能具有可信度。

00:24:07.110 --> 00:24:31.500
<v Nadia Reiman>In January, immigration agents killed Renee Good and then Alex Pretti. That same month, Ryan started talking to congressional staffers about going public, testifying to Congress. He was going to take some time to relocate his family, and then he'd resign from the academy. He told his wife, his parents, and his in-laws about his plan, but not all his siblings or extended family.
<v Nadia Reiman>在一月份，移民特工杀害了蕾妮·古德（Renee Good），随后又杀害了亚历克斯·普雷蒂（Alex Pretti）。就在那个月，瑞安开始与国会工作人员接触，谈论公开此事、向国会作证。他打算花点时间重新安置他的家人，然后他就会从学院辞职。他把这个计划告诉了他的妻子、父母和岳父母，但并没有告诉他所有的兄弟姐妹或大家庭成员。

00:24:31.500 --> 00:24:53.140
<v Ryan Schwank>Part of it was I don't want to burden them with certain things. And then also, you have to keep in mind that I was working for the Department of Homeland Security. I was working for the largest law enforcement agency in the United States. I, frankly, wasn't confident that I could communicate with my family over the phone without the conversations getting flagged.
<v Ryan Schwank>部分原因是我不想用某些事情去加重他们的负担。此外，你还得记住，我当时是在为国土安全部工作。我是在为美国最大的执法机构工作。坦白说，我并没有信心在与家人通电话时，对话不会被标记。

00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:55.520
<v Nadia Reiman>Really?
<v Nadia Reiman>真的吗？

00:24:55.520 --> 00:25:08.460
<v Ryan Schwank>And I don't want to sound paranoid. I want to sound like, oh, I think they're monitoring everybody. I don't know. But the problem is I don't know. It was precautionary, if you will. I just wasn't willing to have certain conversations over an open line.
<v Ryan Schwank>我不想听起来像个偏执狂。我不想表现得好像“哦，我觉得他们在监控所有人”。我不知道。但问题是我不知道。如果你愿意的话，这只是一种预防措施。我只是不愿意在公开线路上进行某些对话。

00:25:08.460 --> 00:25:13.880
<v Nadia Reiman>Ryan testified on February 23, not even two weeks ago, as I'm recording this.
<v Nadia Reiman>瑞安是在2月23日作证的，就在我录音的不到两周前。

00:25:13.880 --> 00:25:26.530
<v Ryan Schwank>Thank you, Senator Blumenthal, Representative Garcia, members of the committee. My name is Ryan Schwank. I swore an oath to uphold the Constitution when I joined ICE on August 1, 2021.
<v Ryan Schwank>谢谢你，布卢门塔尔参议员、加西亚众议员、委员会的各位成员。我叫瑞安·施万克（Ryan Schwank）。我在2021年8月1日加入ICE时，曾宣誓拥护宪法。

00:25:26.530 --> 00:25:58.930
<v Ryan Schwank>Look, I don't think anybody walks away from community they were a member of, the social community they were a member of, lightly. I would have told you six months ago that I had strong friendships with some of the attorneys I worked with, that I had officers that I could go and have a beer with, people that were part of my life that I fully expect are never going to speak to me again.
<v Ryan Schwank>听着，我认为没有人会轻易离开自己曾经所属的社区，他们曾经所属的社交社区。就在六个月前，我会告诉你我和一些共事的律师有着深厚的友谊，我有一些可以一起去喝一杯的警官，那些曾是我生活一部分的人，我完全预计他们再也不会跟我说话了。

00:25:58.930 --> 00:26:05.770
<v Nadia Reiman>Oh, Ryan, I didn't realize that you felt like you had lost your community.
<v Nadia Reiman>噢，瑞安，我没意识到你觉得你失去了自己的社区。

00:26:05.770 --> 00:26:40.310
<v Ryan Schwank>Well, it's that. It's the combination of uncertainty going forward. I have no idea what I'm going to do next. I have a child. I have a wife. I have a family. I walked away from a well-paid, secure career, doing something that I thought was good, into a world where I have no job security. I have no idea what the future holds.
<v Ryan Schwank>嗯，就是这样。这是对未来的不确定性的结合。我完全不知道接下来要做什么。我有一个孩子。我有一个妻子。我有一个家庭。我离开了一份薪水丰厚、安全稳定、做着我认为是好事的工作，走进了一个我没有任何工作安全感的世界。我完全不知道未来会怎样。

00:26:40.310 --> 00:27:09.670
<v Ryan Schwank>I told my wife when I walked off the Senate floor the-- the words that kept going through my head was, and now, for the rest of my life-- sorry. I didn't think this conversation would make me emotional, but it does.
<v Ryan Schwank>我走下参议院发言台时对妻子说——那些不断在我脑海中盘旋的话是：从今以后，在我的余生里——对不起。我没想到这次谈话会让我情绪激动，但确实如此。

00:27:09.670 --> 00:27:10.730
<v Nadia Reiman>It's OK.
<v Nadia Reiman>没关系。

00:27:10.730 --> 00:27:11.760
<v Ryan Schwank>I can just have a second?
<v Ryan Schwank>我能稍微平静一下吗？

00:27:11.760 --> 00:27:14.420
<v Nadia Reiman>Of course, of course. Take your time.
<v Nadia Reiman>当然，当然。慢慢来。

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:16.820
<v Ira Glass>We took a small break.
<v Ira Glass>我们稍微休息了一下。

00:27:16.820 --> 00:27:18.270
<v Nadia Reiman>Hello.
<v Nadia Reiman>你好。

00:27:18.270 --> 00:27:49.020
<v Ryan Schwank>I want to say something real quick before we keep going. For the last four and a half years, this has been my life, right? But I'm the son of an immigrant. My father is a doctor from Guatemala who came to the US to practice. And because he came here to practice from Guatemala, he had a fantastic life. He had a very successful career as a neurosurgeon. I grew up with hundreds of immigrant friends and family members.
<v Ryan Schwank>在我们继续之前，我想快速说几句话。在过去四年半的时间里，这就是我的生活，对吧？但我是个移民的儿子。我父亲是一位来自危地马拉的医生，他来美国行医。正因为他从危地马拉来到这里行医，他过上了非常棒的生活。他作为一名神经外科医生，有着非常成功的职业生涯。我在数百名移民朋友和家人的陪伴下长大。

00:27:49.020 --> 00:28:26.830
<v Ryan Schwank>When I took this job, I gave up the tribe I had before. I lost a lot of friends over my decision to go work for ICE. And that was a hard transition too. And I know that that's one of the things that has been said about me is, oh, he's doing this because he was a plant in the agency or he was just a disgruntled employee. I'm not. I chose the path I took with clear eyes, despite the personal cost to me when I chose it. And that was a price I was willing to pay. That was--
<v Ryan Schwank>当我接受这份工作时，我放弃了我以前的圈子。因为我决定去为ICE工作，我失去了很多朋友。那也是一段艰难的转型期。我知道关于我曾经有过这样一种说法，哦，他是因为受雇于该机构潜伏进来，或者他只是一个心怀不满的员工。我不是。我在选择我走这条路时是清醒的，尽管我为此付出了个人代价。那是甘愿付出的代价。那是——

00:28:26.830 --> 00:28:27.330
<v Nadia Reiman>Why?
<v Nadia Reiman>为什么？

00:28:27.010 --> 00:28:42.790
<v Ryan Schwank>--the thing I was willing to do. Because I genuinely viewed it as the right thing to do.
<v Ryan Schwank>——我愿意做的事情。因为我真心认为那是正确的事情。

00:28:42.790 --> 00:28:54.810
<v Ryan Schwank>I cannot emphasize this enough. What's coming down the road from what I saw at that academy is a clear and direct threat to everyone in this country.
<v Ryan Schwank>我怎么强调都不为过。从我在那所学院看到的情况来看，即将发生的事情对这个国家的每个人都是一个清晰且直接的威胁。

00:28:54.810 --> 00:29:26.040
<v Ryan Schwank>Those cadets have every intention of being good law enforcement officers, but they are being set up to fail. They are being set up to be a danger to the rest of us. I cannot emphasize enough not only the loss of use-of-force training and the firearms training but how critically dangerous it is that we have stripped away the training on the Constitution.
<v Ryan Schwank>那些学员绝对有心成为优秀的执法人员，但他们却被设计成注定要失败。他们被设计成了对我们其余人的威胁。我怎么强调都不为过，不仅是武力使用培训和枪支培训的缺失，更可怕的是，我们剥夺了宪法培训，这是极其危险的。

00:29:26.040 --> 00:30:06.790
<v Ryan Schwank>The law is slow. The courts are a tool for correcting things down the road. The thing that keeps you safe, that keeps me safe, is those police officers buying into that myth of the avatar of justice. And look, I'm not saying this as some naive Boy Scout. I know exactly how our law enforcement operated in the '60s and '70s. I know exactly how it operates today. I'm not saying this because I believe that they're all great, wonderful people. They're human beings with the whole range of character traits and flaws that human beings have.
<v Ryan Schwank>法律是缓慢的。法院是事后纠正问题的工具。让你安全的，让我安全的，是那些警察对正义化身这一神话的信仰。听着，我这样说并不是出于什么天真的童子军心态。我完全清楚我们当年的执法人员在60和70年代是如何运作的。我也完全清楚它今天的运作方式。我这样说并不是因为我认为他们都是伟大、美妙的人。他们只是普通人，拥有普通人所具备的全套性格特征和缺点。

00:30:06.790 --> 00:30:38.750
<v Ryan Schwank>But that idea that they are holding up a standard is so much more powerful than we realize because it's the thing that convinces them to be lawful. That Jiminy Cricket voice in the back of their heads that says, hey, this isn't what an American police officer does, it's the thing that is in their head at that moment that they act.
<v Ryan Schwank>但他们坚守某种标准的这种理念，比我们意识到的要强大得多，因为正是这种理念说服了他们去遵守法律。他们脑海深处的那个小蟋蟀声音在说，嘿，这不是一个美国警察该做的事，这正是他们在采取行动的那一瞬间浮现在脑海中的东西。

00:30:38.750 --> 00:31:05.730
<v Nadia Reiman>In lots of ways, Ryan's unusual, but in one important way, he's not. He sounds just as alarmed about ICE as other people inside the government I've talked to. The Department of Homeland Security plans to train about 3,000 new ICE officers at the academy this year.
<v Nadia Reiman>在很多方面，瑞安很特别，但在一个重要方面，他并不特别。听起来，他和我在政府内部交谈过的其他人一样，对移民及海关执法局（ICE）感到恐慌。国土安全部计划今年在学院培训大约3,000名新的ICE官员。

00:31:05.730 --> 00:31:33.400
<v Ira Glass>Nadia Reiman is a producer on our show. Her story was edited by Laura Starecheski. Coming up, a judge issues some court orders, tells the government to release a bunch of people from detention. The government doesn't do it. The judge calls them in to ask why not. And what the lawyer says was not what he or anybody expected. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>娜迪亚·雷曼是我们节目的制作人。她的报道由劳拉·斯塔雷切斯基剪辑。接下来，一位法官发布了一些法院命令，要求政府释放被关押的一群人。政府没有照做。法官把他们叫来询问原因。而律师的回答既不是他也不是任何人所预料的。芝加哥公共广播电台将在我们的节目继续时带来这部分内容。

00:31:33.400 --> 00:32:01.810
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. OK. So we've named our program today after the song that Jiminy Cricket sings to Pinocchio, "Give A Little Whistle." You know that one? When you get in trouble and you don't know right from wrong, give a little whistle. [WHISTLES] We have two stories today where lawyers from inside the government give us rare and factual accounts of what is really happening inside ICE with fascinating details of a broken, chaotic kind of mess.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。好的。所以我们将今天的节目命名为吉米尼·蟋蟀唱给匹诺曹的歌——《吹一声口哨》。你知道那首歌吗？当你陷入困境，分不清是非对错时，吹一声口哨。[口哨声] 我们今天的两则报道中，来自政府内部的律师向我们罕见而真实地讲述了ICE内部真实发生的情况，并提供了关于这种崩溃、混乱局面的迷人细节。

00:32:01.810 --> 00:32:28.920
<v Ira Glass>We have arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, Don't Trust the Process. So this next glimpse inside the world of ICE comes from a transcript of a court hearing that happened last month. In it, this government attorney got increasingly kind of beaten down by the facts, and by the end, really opened up about what it's like to do her job right now. You do not hear government lawyers talking in court the way she did in this hearing. She's very candid, very unguarded, very personal.
<v Ira Glass>我们迎来了节目的第二幕。第二幕：不要盲目信任程序。接下来对ICE世界的惊鸿一瞥，来自上个月举行的一场听证会的庭审记录。在这场听证会上，这位政府律师被事实打击得渐渐招架不住，并在最后真正敞开心扉，谈到了她现在做这份工作的心情。你平时是绝对听不到政府律师在法庭上说出像她在这次听证会上那样的话的。她非常坦率，毫无防备，非常个人化。

00:32:28.920 --> 00:32:55.680
<v Ira Glass>The reason for the hearing in the first place was kind of notable. Basically, there's been an explosion of what are called habeas petitions in immigration cases. A habeas petition is saying to the government show why this person is being detained. Otherwise, you have to let them go. This is a bedrock legal principle that's been around since the Middle Ages to keep people from being locked up without any reason. It's in the first article of the US Constitution.
<v Ira Glass>这场听证会召开的原因本身就有点引人注目。基本上，移民案件中所谓的“人身保护令”（habeas petitions）呈爆发式增长。人身保护令是要求政府出示羁押此人的理由。否则，你必须放人。这是自中世纪以来就存在的一项根基深厚的法律原则，旨在防止人们在毫无理由的情况下被关押。它被写入了美国宪法第一条。

00:32:55.680 --> 00:33:25.830
<v Ira Glass>ProPublica has been tracking the numbers in immigration cases. Before President Trump came back into office, there were, like, 20 or 30 immigration habeas petitions being filed every week. In February, it was over 2,000 every week, which has completely overwhelmed the federal courts. So the judge in this case had ruled in a bunch of these habeas cases, these people have been illegally detained. You've got to release them immediately. And then it didn't happen. David Kestenbaum has our story.
<v Ira Glass>ProPublica 一直在跟踪移民案件中的数字。在特朗普总统重返白宫之前，每周大约有20或30起移民人身保护令申请被提交。而在2月份，这个数字超过了每周2,000起，这彻底压垮了联邦法院。因此，本案的法官在其中的一连串人身保护令案件中作出裁决：这些人被非法羁押了。你们必须立即释放他们。然而事情并没有发生。大卫·克斯滕鲍姆为我们带来这则报道。

00:33:25.830 --> 00:33:46.290
<v David Kestenbaum>This all happened 1 o'clock on a Tuesday afternoon at a federal courtroom in Saint Paul, Minnesota. There are just a handful of people there. The judge, Judge Jerry Blackwell, ordered the hearing because he was trying to figure out what on Earth was going on with the government's lawyers. We got an actor to read the transcript here for you. It's been lightly edited, but just for clarity and length.
<v David Kestenbaum>这一切发生在周二下午1点，地点是明尼苏达州圣保罗的一间联邦法庭。里面只有寥寥数人。法官杰里·布莱克威尔法官下令召开听证会，因为他试图弄清楚政府律师到底在搞什么鬼。我们找了一位演员在这里为您朗读庭审记录。内容经过了轻微剪辑，纯粹是为了清晰和精简。

00:33:46.290 --> 00:34:03.100
<v Rich Sommer>Judge Blackwell-- good afternoon. You may be seated. The hearing this afternoon concerns compliance with court orders-- not policy, just compliance, nothing else. I've had so many issues with noncompliance in just this past week that I called for this hearing.
<v Rich Sommer>布莱克威尔法官——下午好。请坐。今天下午的听证会关乎对法院命令的遵守——不是政策问题，仅仅是遵守，没有别的。就在过去这一周，我遇到了太多不遵守命令的问题，所以我才召集了这场听证会。

00:34:03.100 --> 00:34:34.540
<v David Kestenbaum>The judge's issue is with five different immigration cases. The court had ruled that the people the government had detained had to be released because the government had no reason to hold them. But then the government was not releasing them or even giving updates. Hence, this hearing. And you know how in some TV shows or movies, there's this moment where someone will give this kind of speech? And it's good, but you're like, people don't actually do that in real life. Well, sometimes they do. It's like Judge Blackwell knew exactly what he wanted to say, and it all came out.
<v David Kestenbaum>法官关注的是五个不同的移民案件。法院裁定，政府羁押的人必须被释放，因为政府没有任何理由继续关押他们。但随后政府却没有释放他们，甚至连最新进展都不提供。因此才有了这场听证会。你知道在某些电视剧或电影里，总会有某个时刻，某人会发表这样一番慷慨激词吗？情节很好，但你会觉得，现实生活中人们根本不会那样做。嗯，有时候他们还真会。就像布莱克威尔法官清楚地知道自己想说些什么，然后一切就自然而然地倾泻而出了。

00:34:34.540 --> 00:34:58.280
<v Rich Sommer>As I hope everybody here agrees and acknowledges-- that a court order is not advisory and it is not conditional. It is not something that any agency can treat as optional. The authority exercised by the court is derived from Article III of the Constitution. That authority under Article III only has meaning if the court orders are obeyed, adhered to promptly, fully, and in good faith.
<v Rich Sommer>正如我希望这里所有人都同意并承认的那样——法院命令不是建议性的，也不是有条件的。它绝不是任何机构可以视为可有可无的东西。法院行使的权力源自宪法第三条。宪法第三条赋予的这种权力，只有在法院命令得到遵守、得到迅速、完全且出于善意地执行时，才具有意义。

00:34:58.280 --> 00:35:16.340
<v David Kestenbaum>The whole judicial system kind of depends on the executive branch doing what the courts say, more or less voluntarily. He doesn't have a great way to force them to do it. The judge says, if you don't comply with the court's orders, you've essentially painted the court into a corner. Because what are we supposed to do?
<v David Kestenbaum>整个司法体系在某种程度上依赖于行政部门或多或少自愿地执行法院的判决。他并没有什么绝佳的办法强迫他们去执行。法官说，如果你们不遵守法院的命令，你们基本上就把法院逼入了绝境。因为我们到底该怎么做？

00:35:16.340 --> 00:35:54.760
<v Rich Sommer>Detention without lawful authority is not just a technical defect. It is a constitutional injury that unfairly falls on the heads of those who have done nothing wrong to justify it. The overwhelming majority of the hundreds seen by this court have been found to be lawfully present as of now in the country. They live in their communities. Some are separated from their families. When a release order is not followed, the result is not just a delay. In some instances, it is the continued detention of a person the Constitution does not permit the government to hold and who should have been left alone-- that is, not arrested in the first place.
<v Rich Sommer>在没有合法授权的情况下进行羁押，不仅仅是一个技术缺陷。这是一种宪法伤害，不公平地降临在那些没有做错任何事情来为此辩护的人头上。法官发现，这数百人中的绝大多数目前在该国都是合法居留。他们生活在各自的社区里。有些人与家人骨肉分离。当释放令未被执行时，造成的后果不仅仅是拖延。在某些情况下，这是对宪法不允许政府关押、本应被放过——也就是说，一开始就不应该被逮捕——的人的持续羁押。

00:35:54.760 --> 00:36:06.170
<v David Kestenbaum>I can't tell how long, time wise, this goes on for, because we just have the transcript. But it's, like, five pages. He says the court has been having a hard time even getting information about when these people would be released.
<v David Kestenbaum>我无法判断这持续了多长时间，因为我们只有文字记录。但这大约有五页纸。他说，法院甚至很难获得关于这些人何时会被释放的信息。

00:36:06.170 --> 00:36:39.590
<v Rich Sommer>In many instances, I have had to not just issue an order but another order, another order. Another order, about seven or eight different touches sent to the government, simply asking for the date, time, and location of the release of someone who was ordered released, in many instances, a week or more in the past. And why is that so difficult? I cannot understand. Because there's obviously a person associated with the government who is going to the detainee to release him or her. You have their name. You can carry with you a form. The name is on it. Just write the time on it and send it to the DOJ.
<v Rich Sommer>在许多情况下，我不得不不仅下一道命令，还要下达另一道命令、又一道命令。又一道命令，向政府发出了大约七八次催问，仅仅是要求提供被下令释放的某人的释放日期、时间和地点——而在许多情况下，这个人本应在一周或更早以前就被释放了。为什么这会如此困难？我无法理解。因为很显然，政府有一个相关人员正前往被羁押者那里去释放他或她。你们有他们的名字。你们可以随身携带一份表格。名字就在上面。把时间写在上面，然后发给司法部。

00:36:39.590 --> 00:36:52.870
<v David Kestenbaum>Judge Blackwell says to the government lawyers, there's no excuse to say that you don't have the staff to deal with this huge influx of habeas cases, because it was the government that decided to do all these immigration raids with apparently no plan for how to deal with them in the court system.
<v David Kestenbaum>布莱克威尔法官对政府律师说，没有任何借口可以说你们没有工作人员来应对这起海量的人身保护令案件，因为正是政府决定开展所有这些移民突袭行动，却显然没有制定任何如何在法院系统中处理这些案件的计划。

00:36:52.870 --> 00:37:01.980
<v Rich Sommer>So I'm going to stop there just by way of background and follow up on the responses I received from Ms. Le and from Ms. Voss. And if you want to come up to the podium, Ms. Le
<v Rich Sommer>所以我先讲到这里作为背景介绍，接下来跟进我从勒女士（Ms. Le）和沃斯女士（Ms. Voss）那里收到的答复。如果你想走到讲台前来，勒女士。

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:05.600
<v David Kestenbaum>Ms. Le is Julie Le, an attorney for ICE.
<v David Kestenbaum>勒女士是朱莉·勒（Julie Le），一名ICE的律师。

00:37:05.600 --> 00:37:08.420
<v Rich Sommer>Ms. Le-- Your Honor, may I approach?
<v Rich Sommer>勒女士——法官大人，我可以走近发言吗？

00:37:08.420 --> 00:37:11.440
<v Rich Sommer>Judge Blackwell-- you have approached already.
<v Rich Sommer>布莱克威尔法官——你已经走过来了。

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:14.700
<v David Kestenbaum>She presents some documents to the judge and starts talking.
<v David Kestenbaum>她向法官递交了一些文件，并开始说话。

00:37:14.700 --> 00:37:44.100
<v Rich Sommer>Ms. Le-- just to have some background, I was put on this special mission to help with the US Attorney Office with all the habeas claims that they have received. They are overwhelmed, and they need help, so I-- I have to say, stupidly enough to volunteer. So in January 5, when I started with the agency, I have to be honest, we have no guidance or direction on what we need to do. And so when you showed up, they just throw you in the well and then here we go.
<v Rich Sommer>勒女士——为了说明一些背景，我被安排执行这个特殊任务，协助美国检察官办公室处理他们收到的所有人身保护令诉求。他们人手严重不足，需要帮助，所以我就——我必须得说，愚蠢到主动请缨。因此，在1月5日我加入该机构时，老实说，我们没有获得关于我们需要做什么的任何指导或方向。所以当你出现时，他们只是把你扔进深水区，然后我们就这样上了。

00:37:44.100 --> 00:38:00.710
<v David Kestenbaum>She explains that it's been kind of a mess. Like, she has two different government email accounts now but could only access one. Some of the court's orders were going to the other email that she couldn't access. She's spending hours and hours just not even doing the job, just trying to figure out how to do the job.
<v David Kestenbaum>她解释说，情况简直一团糟。比如，她现在有两个不同的政府电子邮箱账号，但只能访问其中一个。法院的一些命令发到了她无法访问的另一个邮箱里。她花费了大量时间甚至都不是在工作，而只是在摸索如何开展工作。

00:38:00.710 --> 00:38:14.130
<v Rich Sommer>Judge Blackwell-- so are you telling the court that you were brought in brand new, a shiny, brand-new penny into this role, and you received no proper orientation or training on what you were supposed to do?
<v Rich Sommer>布莱克威尔法官——所以你是告诉法庭，你作为全新的一员、一个崭新的面孔被带到了这个岗位上，却没有接受任何关于你应该做什么的适当岗前培训或指导？

00:38:14.130 --> 00:38:18.810
<v Rich Sommer>Ms. Le-- I have to say yes to that question, Your Honor.
<v Rich Sommer>勒女士——对于这个问题，我不得不说是的，法官大人。

00:38:18.810 --> 00:38:24.770
<v Rich Sommer>Judge Blackwell-- I'd like to hear about the Oscar case, Oscar 26-167.
<v Rich Sommer>布莱克威尔法官——我想听听关于奥斯卡案的情况，奥斯卡 26-167。

00:38:24.770 --> 00:38:54.860
<v David Kestenbaum>The Oscar case is the main one discussed in the transcript. The details of it are particularly troubling to the judge. Oscar was apprehended on January 10 in Minneapolis, where he lives. Five days later, on the 15th, the court ordered him to be immediately released. But by then-- this is very common-- Oscar been flown all the way from Minnesota to a detention facility in Texas. The judge, when ordering his immediate release on the 15th, required an update to be filed within 48 hours.
<v David Kestenbaum>奥斯卡案是庭审记录中讨论的主要案件。案件的细节让法官尤为感到不安。奥斯卡于1月10日在他的居住地明尼阿波利斯被捕。五天后的15日，法院下令立即释放他。但到那时——这种情况非常普遍——奥斯卡已经被用飞机从明尼苏达州一路押送到了得克萨斯州的一个羁押设施。法官在15日下令立即释放他时，要求在48小时内提交一份最新进展报告。

00:38:54.860 --> 00:39:22.440
<v Rich Sommer>Judge Blackwell-- there was no 48-hour update that was filed on the 17th. That hadn't happened. And so then the court is saying that I wanted a letter no later than 3:00 PM on the 19th showing cause why you shouldn't be held in contempt for violating the court's order. Then on the 19th, I'm told the petitioner was scheduled for return to Minnesota on a flight from El Paso on Tuesday, January 20, and that he would be released upon his return.
<v Rich Sommer>布莱克威尔法官——17日并没有提交48小时的更新报告。那并没有发生。因此，法庭随后表示，我要求在19日下午3点之前收到一封信函，说明为什么不应以违反法院命令为由判处你们藐视法庭。然后到了19日，我被告知申请人计划于1月20日星期二乘坐从埃尔帕索起飞的航班返回明尼苏达州，并且他将在返回后被释放。

00:39:22.440 --> 00:39:24.400
<v David Kestenbaum>Petitioner here just means Oscar.
<v David Kestenbaum>这里的“申请人”指的就是奥斯卡。

00:39:24.400 --> 00:40:02.220
<v Rich Sommer>That was on the 19th of January. Then we learned that on the 20th, when the release was supposed to have taken place, there was no release. Instead, we're told that counsel for ICE had represented to petitioner's counsel that the petitioner was currently on his route stop in Albuquerque, New Mexico, not El Paso, Texas, and is scheduled to arrive in Saint Paul on Saturday the 24th. And this is already then nine days after this person has been ordered released and found to have been unlawfully detained in the first place. So nine days later, he's still in custody, now being flown around from El Paso to Albuquerque, New Mexico.
<v Rich Sommer>那是1月9日（注：应为1月19日）。然后我们得知，在应当完成释放的20日，并没有发生释放。相反，我们被告知，ICE的法律顾问向申请人的律师表示，申请人目前正在新墨西哥州阿尔伯克基的中转站，而不是得克萨斯州的埃尔帕索，预计将于24日星期六抵达圣保罗。而此时，距离这个人被下令释放并被认定为最初遭到非法羁押，已经过去整整九天了。所以九天后，他仍然被羁押着，现在正被用飞机从埃尔帕索辗转运送到新墨西哥州的阿尔伯克基。

00:40:02.220 --> 00:40:08.160
<v David Kestenbaum>At this point, a lawyer for Oscar, the man who is still somehow being held, filed a motion for contempt of court.
<v David Kestenbaum>此时，奥斯卡的律师——那个至今仍被某种方式关押的人的律师——提交了一份藐视法庭的动议。

00:40:08.160 --> 00:40:12.900
<v Rich Sommer>Judge Blackwell-- I ordered then Ms. Voss to file a written response
<v Rich Sommer>布莱克威尔法官——我随后命令沃斯女士提交书面答复。

00:40:12.900 --> 00:40:20.780
<v David Kestenbaum>Ms. Voss is Ana Voss, a senior lawyer representing the federal government here. She's in the courtroom, listening to all of this.
<v David Kestenbaum>沃斯女士是安娜·沃斯（Ana Voss），在此代表联邦政府的高级律师。她当时正在法庭里，听着这一切。

00:40:20.780 --> 00:41:04.490
<v Rich Sommer>I'm sure she's had more than one day where she regrets how well I know her name, since most things end in, get a hold of Ms. Voss. Ms. Voss responded on the 22nd, explaining the timing of when she learned the facts. And let me pause there to make another point kind of clear enough. I wholeheartedly embrace the notion of a unitary executive, as in DHS, ICE, the DOJ, all a part of the executive branch. And if there's a problem in the restaurant, I don't intend to go in the kitchen to try to figure out who makes the bread. And all of it is part of the executive branch. And so it is not an excuse to tell me you contacted ICE because ICE is also part of the unitary executive for accountability purposes.
<v Rich Sommer>我敢肯定，她不止有一天会后悔我怎么把她的名字记得这么熟，因为大多数事情最后都以“去找沃斯女士”告终。沃斯女士在22日作出了回应，解释了她得知事实的时间点。在这里我停顿一下，把另一个观点阐述得足够清楚。我完全拥护单一行政主管（unitary executive）的概念，即国土安全部、ICE、司法部，全都是行政部门的一部分。如果餐馆里出了问题，我不打算走进厨房去弄清楚是谁做的面包。所有这些都是行政部门的一部分。因此，告诉我你联系了ICE并不能成为借口，因为出于问责目的，ICE同样是单一行政主管的一部分。

00:41:04.490 --> 00:41:29.050
<v David Kestenbaum>After Ms. Voss's response, more days pass, with the man, Oscar, still in detention and the judge not getting updates. Apparently, in some of the filings back and forth, the government had said that the delay in releasing this man, Oscar, had something to do with safety concerns. The judge had asked, What were the safety concerns? Three times. In court, after a while, he finally got an answer.
<v David Kestenbaum>在沃斯女士回应之后，好几天过去了，名叫奥斯卡的男子依然被羁押，法官也没有收到最新进展。显然，在来来回回的一些法律文件中，政府曾表示，推迟释放这名男子奥斯卡与安全隐患有关。法官曾三次追问：安全隐患是什么？在法庭上僵持了一段时间后，他终于得到了一个答案。

00:41:29.050 --> 00:41:58.120
<v Rich Sommer>Ms. Le-- the reason that I-- I did ask. And I was told if we provided all information, the protesters would show up at the airport and the agent and other people will be endangered. So I took it to heart, because during that time, it was very heated here in Minnesota with all the protests that was going on. Any public thing that was going on is at risk, even myself also at risk for putting my name and myself in here, Your Honor. That's the safety concern that I have.
<v Rich Sommer>勒女士——我问过原因——我被告知，如果我们提供所有信息，抗议者就会出现在机场，特工和其他人的生命安全将受到威胁。所以我把这话听进去了，因为当时明尼苏达州的情况非常紧张，到处都是抗议活动。任何正在进行的公众活动都面临风险，法官大人，甚至我自己把名字和本人牵涉进来也面临风险。这就是我所担忧的安全问题。

00:41:58.120 --> 00:42:07.040
<v Rich Sommer>Judge Blackwell-- is your answer because they were concerned that if he were put on a plane, that if he arrived here, there may have been a public reaction of some kind?
<v Rich Sommer>布莱克威尔法官——你的回答是因为他们担心，如果把他送上飞机，如果他抵达这里，可能会引发某种公众反应？

00:42:07.040 --> 00:42:24.840
<v Rich Sommer>Ms. Le-- Your Honor, he was escorted with other agents. He wasn't put on the plane by himself. The original plan was to have agent escorted him back. And with the protests was going on during that time, I was advised to be careful of what information to put out in public for the safety of others.
<v Rich Sommer>勒女士——法官大人，他是与其他特工一起被护送的。他不是独自一人被塞上飞机的。最初的计划是让特工护送他回去。鉴于当时正在发生的抗议活动，我被建议为了他人的安全，在公开发布什么信息时要小心谨慎。

00:42:24.840 --> 00:42:37.200
<v Rich Sommer>Judge Blackwell-- and when the various persons are detained and then flown to El Paso or New Mexico, do you know that they are flown out of Minnesota sometimes within hours, if not the next day of being detained?
<v Rich Sommer>布莱克威尔法官——当各色人员被羁押然后用飞机运往埃尔帕索或新墨西哥州时，你知道有时他们在被羁押后的几个小时内，甚至第二天就被用飞机运出了明尼苏达州吗？

00:42:37.200 --> 00:42:40.240
<v Rich Sommer>Ms. Le-- yes, I know that they are doing that.
<v Rich Sommer>勒女士——是的，我知道他们在这样做。

00:42:40.240 --> 00:42:47.230
<v Rich Sommer>Judge Blackwell-- and do you feel there's anything wrong with taking hours or days to fly them out of Minnesota when they've been--
<v Rich Sommer>布莱克威尔法官——那你觉得花几个小时或几天把他们用飞机运出明尼苏达州，而当他们被发现遭到非法羁押后——

00:42:47.230 --> 00:42:49.630
<v Rich Sommer>Ms. Le-- oh, yeah, definitely.
<v Rich Sommer>勒女士——哦，是的，绝对有问题。

00:42:49.630 --> 00:42:59.950
<v Rich Sommer>Judge Blackwell-- just a minute. Let me finish my question. To fly them out of Minnesota and then take 13 days to return them when they've been found to have been unlawfully detained--
<v Rich Sommer>布莱克威尔法官——等一下。让我把问题问完。把他们用飞机运出明尼苏达州，然后在被发现遭到非法羁押后，却要花上13天才能把他们送回来——

00:42:59.950 --> 00:43:06.750
<v Rich Sommer>Ms. Le-- Your Honor, I did ask the same question too. I have not got the answer.
<v Rich Sommer>勒女士……法官阁下，我也问过同样的问题。我一直没有得到答案。

00:43:06.750 --> 00:43:42.500
<v David Kestenbaum>If there is a person who can answer that question or explain or defend the government's actions, it does not seem like they are in this courtroom. Judge Blackwell and Julie Le, who is supposed to be representing the government, they agree this is a problem. The judge is saying, this looks bad from the outside. And Julie Le is like, looks bad on the inside. And here, the whole thing kind of goes off the rails a bit. Julie Le confesses to the judge that at one point, she put in her resignation, but the government couldn't find a replacement. She said she was just going to walk out, but then she got another person released, a kid, someone under 18.
<v David Kestenbaum>如果有一个人能回答那个问题、或者解释或辩护政府的行为，那这个人似乎并不在这个法庭里。布莱克韦尔法官和本应代表政府的朱莉·勒都同意这是一个问题。法官说，这从外表看起来很糟糕。而朱莉·勒则认为，从内部看也很糟糕。就在这里，事情有点彻底失控了。朱莉·勒向法官坦白，她一度提交了辞职信，但政府找不到替代人选。她说她本打算直接走人，但后来她又让另一个人获释了，那是一个孩子，一个未满 18 岁的人。

00:43:42.500 --> 00:44:08.900
<v Rich Sommer>Ms. Le-- that kind of like a step, like a barrier, like, wait, Julie, stop. You need to go back and get more people out. That's why I'm still here. Because if I walk out-- sometimes I wish you would just hold me in contempt, Your Honor, so that I can have a full 24 hours of sleep. I work days and night just because people still in there. And, yes, procedure in place right now sucks. I'm trying to fix it.
<v Rich Sommer>勒女士——这有点像一个台阶，就像一个障碍，就像，等等，朱莉，停下。你需要回去把更多的人弄出来。这就是为什么我还在这里。因为如果我走人——法官阁下，有时我真希望你直接以藐视法庭罪拘留我，这样我就可以睡足整整 24 小时了。我日夜工作，只因为人们还在里面。而且，是的，现在实行的程序糟透了。我在努力修复它。

00:44:08.900 --> 00:44:45.170
<v Rich Sommer>As you can see, the email that I sent to you, it has been improving, a great improvement. And last night, I had to stay up until 2:35 AM just to get these documents ready for you. I can't say it's a waste of my time, but I could have sent so many more emails and get so many more people ready to be released. And I am here with you, Your Honor. What do you want me to do? The system sucks. This job sucks. And I am trying every breath that I have so that I can get you what you need.
<v Rich Sommer>正如你所看到的，我发给你的电子邮件一直在改善，有了很大的改进。昨晚，我不得不熬夜到凌晨 2 点 35 分，只为了为你准备好这些文件。我不能说这是在浪费我的时间，但我本来可以发送更多电子邮件，让更多人准备好获释。法官阁下，我和你在一起。你想让我做什么？这个系统糟透了。这份工作糟透了。我正在用尽我身上的每一口气，以便能为你提供你所需的东西。

00:44:45.170 --> 00:45:03.700
<v David Kestenbaum>The judge asks Le if she is expressing this frustration to other people at ICE or DOJ. Yes, she says. I write emails in 24-point font, bold. I threaten that I will put their names on the court documents. Judge Blackwell tells Le he doesn't want to threaten her or anyone. He just wants this fixed.
<v David Kestenbaum>法官问勒，她是否在向移民及海关执法局（ICE）或司法部（DOJ）其他人表达这种挫败感。是的，她说。我用 24 号加粗字体写电子邮件。我威胁说要把他们的名字写进法庭文件里。布莱克韦尔法官告诉勒，他不想威胁她或任何人。他只想解决这个问题。

00:45:03.700 --> 00:45:39.110
<v Rich Sommer>Judge Blackwell-- what we really want is simply compliance. Because on the other side of this is somebody who should not have been arrested in some instances in the first place, who is being held in jail or put in shackles for days, if not a week plus after they've been ordered released. That's my concern-- upholding the rule of law and the constitutional rights of all concerned. I hear the concerns about all the energy that this is causing the DOJ to expend, but with respect, some of it is of your own making by not complying with orders. Do you understand that?
<v Rich Sommer>布莱克韦尔法官——我们真正想要的只是合规。因为在这件事的另一端，有些人根本就不应该被捕，他们被关在监狱里，或者戴上手铐几天，甚至在被下令释放后达一周多。这是我的关切——维护法治和所有相关人员的宪法权利。我听到有人对司法部因此耗费的大量精力表示担忧，但恕我直言，其中一些是你们自己造成的，因为你们没有遵守命令。你明白这一点吗？

00:45:39.110 --> 00:46:13.630
<v Rich Sommer>Ms. Le-- I do, and I share the same concern with you, Your Honor. I am not white, as you can see. And my family's at risk as any other people that might get picked up too. So I share the same concern. Our email just never stops. And as you can see, I would love to undo all of this stuff because no one wants to be in jail. And actually, honestly, being in jail a day to get-- catch up with sleep is not bad right now with all the hours I have to put into this job.
<v Rich Sommer>勒女士——我明白，法官阁下，我和您有同样的担忧。如您所见，我不是白人。我的家人也面临着和其他可能被抓的人一样的风险。所以我有同样的担忧。我们的电子邮件从未停止过。如您所见，我很乐意撤销所有这些事情，因为没有人愿意蹲监狱。说实话，考虑到我为这份工作投入的大量时间，现在坐一天牢来补觉其实也不算太坏。

00:46:13.630 --> 00:46:23.970
<v Rich Sommer>Judge Blackwell-- Ms. Le, I appreciate your candor.
<v Rich Sommer>布莱克韦尔法官——勒女士，我很赞赏你的坦诚。

00:46:23.970 --> 00:47:02.940
<v David Kestenbaum>That's basically where the transcript ends. What to make of all this? You could say this is a document about the chaos of this moment and how the legal process is not working. The judge had to call this hearing, after all. But he did call it, and we have the record of it. And reading it, I kept thinking about Oscar. Because while all this was unfolding, all those days Judge Blackwell and Julie Le were talking about and going back and forth about, those were all days in his life, days when, as the judge says, he should not have been detained in the first place. What happened for Oscar?
<v David Kestenbaum>记录基本上就在这里结束了。对此作何感想？你可以说这是一份关于这个混乱时刻以及法律程序如何失灵的文件。毕竟，法官不得不召开这次听证会。但他确实召开了，我们也有了记录。读着它，我一直在想奥斯卡。因为当这一切展开时，布莱克韦尔法官和朱莉·勒谈论并反复争论的那些日子，对他的生活来说全都是那些日子，正如法官所说，在那些日子里，他本来就不应该被拘留。奥斯卡身上发生了什么？

00:47:02.940 --> 00:47:18.380
<v David Kestenbaum>At the end of the hearing, his attorney urges the judge to please read docket entry number 67, which actually answers all these questions. It's worth hearing. So here. We've edited this for length and clarity. Each paragraph is numbered. That's the way things are written.
<v David Kestenbaum>听证会结束时，他的律师敦促法官请阅读案卷条目第 67 号，该条目实际上回答了所有这些问题。听一听很有价值。所以，在这里。我们为控制篇幅和保持清晰度进行了编辑。每个段落都有编号。事情就是这样书写的。

00:47:18.380 --> 00:47:27.340
<v Marquis Rodriguez>Declaration of O. I, O, declare the following is true and accurate to the best of my knowledge and recollection.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>O 的声明。我，O，声明据我所知和记忆所及，以下内容真实准确。

00:47:27.340 --> 00:47:41.090
<v Marquis Rodriguez>1. My name is O. I am a 20-year-old man from Guatemala. I have lived in Minneapolis, Minnesota, for about four years. I have pending asylum and Special Immigration Juvenile Status applications.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>1. 我的名字是 O。我是一名来自危地马拉的 20 岁男子。我在明尼苏达州明尼阿波利斯生活了大约四年。我的庇护和特殊移民青少年身份申请正在审理中。

00:47:41.090 --> 00:47:55.130
<v Marquis Rodriguez>2. I am filing this declaration under my first initial rather than my full name due to fear of retaliation against my family or me for opposing the federal government. My full name is known to plaintiff's counsel.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>2. 由于担心因反对联邦政府而遭到针对我和家人的报复，我以姓氏首字母而非全名提交了这份声明。原告律师知道我的全名。

00:47:55.130 --> 00:48:32.800
<v Marquis Rodriguez>3. On Saturday, January 10, 2026, I went to work and then got lunch with my father and my cousin. We drove back to my work together after that. On the way back, a car passed me quickly, sped up, and stopped in the middle of the street. A few men got out of the car in front of us and ran towards us. I turned off the car. The men told me if I didn't get out of the car, that they would force me out. I got out of the car. They told me I was under arrest but didn't say why. They handcuffed me. I left my keys and phone in the car. I felt like I was being kidnapped.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>3. 2026 年 1 月 10 日，星期六，我去上班，然后和父亲还有表弟一起吃午饭。之后我们一起开车回我的单位。在回来的路上，一辆车飞快地从我身边超过去，加速并在街道中间停了下来。几个男人从我们前面的车里出来，向我们跑来。我熄了火。那些人告诉我，如果我不下车，他们就会强行把我拉出来。我下了车。他们告诉我我被捕了，但没有说原因。他们给我戴上手铐。我的钥匙和手机落在了车里。我觉得自己被绑架了。

00:48:32.800 --> 00:48:39.320
<v Marquis Rodriguez>4. They took us to the same place, the Whipple building, where the immigration court is located.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>4. 他们把我们带到同一个地方，即移民法庭所在的惠普尔大楼。

00:48:39.320 --> 00:49:02.880
<v Marquis Rodriguez>5. In the garage, ICE agents checked our belongings and IDs, and then they took us inside and put us in a cell where we waited to be processed. The cell was small but was already holding about 40 people. We were there close to 20 hours in that cell. It was really hot and dirty. There was food scattered on the floor. The floor was sticky with mud, and everything stuck to your shoes.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>5. 在车库里，移民及海关执法局特工检查了我们的随身物品和身份证件，然后把我们带进去，关进一个等待处理的牢房里。牢房很小，但里面已经关了大约 40 人。我们在那个牢房里待了将近 20 个小时。里面真的很热很脏。地板上散落着食物。地板上沾满泥浆，黏糊糊的，鞋子上沾满了各种东西。

00:49:02.880 --> 00:49:30.500
<v Marquis Rodriguez>I was so tired. There was one toilet, but there was no privacy. I worried about viruses and bacteria since there were so many of us in the cramped space. We were only given food two times in that almost 20 hours, both times a sandwich, an apple, and a cookie. I tried to sleep, but there was no space to sit down. When some people got up from the floor, I would sit down, but it was painful to be on the cement floor.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>我太累了。里面有一个马桶，但毫无隐私可言。由于狭小的空间里挤了这么多人，我担心病毒和细菌。在这将近 20 个小时里，我们只被喂了两次饭，每次都是一个三明治、一个苹果和一块饼干。我试图睡觉，但没有地方坐下。当有些人从地板上站起来时，我会坐下，但坐在水泥地上实在太痛苦了。

00:49:30.500 --> 00:49:54.850
<v Marquis Rodriguez>6. On Sunday, January 11, after breakfast, an ICE officer came and got me. He took my fingerprints. I was finally allowed to make one call, one call, no time limit. I wanted to call my attorney, Kim Boche, but I didn't have her number. The ICE officer said I was being moved to Texas as soon as possible and I will face a judge in Texas.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>6. 1 月 11 日，星期日，早饭后，一名移民及海关执法局官员来找我。他采集了我的指纹。我终于被允许打一个电话，只有一个电话，没有时间限制。我想给我的律师金·博奇打电话，但我没有她的电话号码。移民及海关执法局官员说，我将尽快被转移到德克萨斯州，我将在德克萨斯州面对法官。

00:49:54.850 --> 00:50:09.060
<v Marquis Rodriguez>7. Around 11:00 AM on January 11, an officer gathered everyone in the cell, chained our hands and feet, and we were put in trucks. ICE took us to the airport. There were around 120 detainees on the plane.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>7. 1 月 11 日上午 11 点左右，一名官员把牢房里的所有人召集起来，用铁链锁住我们的手脚，然后把我们塞进卡车。移民及海关执法局把我们带到机场。飞机上大约有 120 名被扣押人员。

00:50:09.060 --> 00:50:23.840
<v Marquis Rodriguez>8. I remained in chains the whole plane ride. I was very hungry. No food or water were offered for a while. I was finally given a snack and water on the plane, about 12 hours since I had last eaten, and had to eat with handcuffs on.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>8. 整个飞行过程中我都戴着铁链。我饿极了。好一会儿没有人提供食物或水。飞机上终于给我发了点心和水，距离我上次进食大约过去了 12 小时，而且我必须戴着手铐吃东西。

00:50:23.840 --> 00:50:40.510
<v Marquis Rodriguez>9. I arrived in El Paso, Texas, around 6:00 or 7:00 PM on January 11. When I arrived at the El Paso, Texas, facility, I was given a small bag with two thin blankets, a towel, two T-shirts, and a pair of boxers.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>9. 1 月 11 日下午 6 点到 7 点左右，我抵达了德克萨斯州埃尔帕索。当我到达德克萨斯州埃尔帕索的设施时，我拿到了一个小袋子，里面有两条薄毯子、一条毛巾、两件 T 恤和一条平角短裤。

00:50:40.510 --> 00:50:49.330
<v Marquis Rodriguez>10. I was told by the security official at the prison that the place I was brought to in Texas was supposed to be temporary, but I spent 10 days there.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>10. 监狱的一名安全官员告诉我，我被带到的德克萨斯州这个地方本应该是临时的，但我却在那里度过了 10 天。

00:50:49.330 --> 00:51:14.530
<v Marquis Rodriguez>12. There were about 72 of us in the cell. The cell was the size of a small gym, where they fit about 40 bunk beds. There were only two flip phones for all the detainees in this center to use. Over the 10 days I was in El Paso, the officers probably brought the phones into the cell for our use two to three times for two hours each. We would get together and beg the officer to let us call our family or our attorneys.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>12. 我们的牢房里大约有 72 人。牢房有小型体育馆那么大，里面塞了大约 40 张双层床。这个中心的所有被扣押人员只有两部翻盖手机可以使用。在我待在埃尔帕索的这 10 天里，官员们大概把手机拿进牢房给我们使用过两到三次，每次两个小时。我们会聚在一起，哀求官员让我们给家人或律师打电话。

00:51:14.530 --> 00:51:36.120
<v Marquis Rodriguez>If we could get the phone, we could only use it for two minutes. You had to be ready when it was your turn. If you didn't have an attorney's number memorized, you couldn't call them. There were signs everywhere in the detention facility offering us $3,000 each if we agreed to self-deport. The officers would frequently try to get people to sign forms agreeing to self-deport.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>如果我们能拿到手机，我们只能使用两分钟。轮到你的时候你必须准备好。如果你没有背熟律师的电话号码，你就没法打电话。拘留所到处都贴着标语，如果我们同意自行遣返，每人将获得 3,000 美元。官员们经常试图让人们签署同意自行遣返的表格。

00:51:36.120 --> 00:52:12.840
<v Marquis Rodriguez>15. After five or six days-- I'm not sure exactly-- I was finally allowed to make a two-minute phone call. I was able to obtain my lawyer's phone number from my dad, who I saw through the window in a different room. I asked the security guard to ask the other security guard to get my lawyer's phone number from my dad. I was finally able to talk to my attorney, Kim Boche, for one minute only. She said she was trying to arrange a call with me. The security guard started yelling at me that my time was up, so I couldn't understand much. I don't remember much of the phone call. I was so nervous.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>15. 五六天后——我记不清具体是几天了——我终于被允许打一个两分钟的电话。我从我爸那里弄到了律师的电话号码，我是通过另一个房间的窗户看到他的。我请保安让另一个保安从我爸那里拿我律师的电话号码。我终于能够和我的律师金·博奇通话了，时间仅限一分钟。她说她正试图安排跟我通话。保安开始对大喊大叫说我的时间到了，所以我没听懂多少。我对那个电话没记清多少。我太紧张了。

00:52:12.840 --> 00:52:37.710
<v Marquis Rodriguez>16. On Saturday the 17th, I talked to my attorney, and she told me I was supposed to be released the next day or the day after that. She told me a judge had ordered my release. I also received two letters and a list with phone numbers in the mail from one of my teachers. She also sent snacks, clothes, and a notebook. But I wasn't allowed to have any of this, only the papers and letters.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>16. 17 日星期六，我和律师通了电话，她告诉我我应该在第二天或后天获释。她告诉我法官已经下令释放我。我还收到了一位老师寄来的两封信和一张写有电话号码的信箱名单。她还寄来了零食、衣服和笔记本。但我什么都不准留，只能留那些文件和信件。

00:52:37.710 --> 00:52:57.950
<v Marquis Rodriguez>18. ICE did not tell me that my attorney had been trying to call me and contact me while I was in Texas. They didn't tell me my attorney had retained another attorney to file a habeas petition on my behalf or that a court had granted it and ordered my release. They just kept holding me there and occasionally trying to get me to self-deport.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>18. 移民及海关执法局没有告诉我在我身处德克萨斯州期间我的律师一直试图打电话和联系我。他们没有告诉我我的律师已经聘请了另一位律师代表我提交人身保护令申请，也没有告诉我法院已经批准了该申请并下令释放我。他们只是把我关在那里，偶尔试图让我自行遣返。

00:52:57.950 --> 00:53:14.030
<v Marquis Rodriguez>19. I was in detention in El Paso, Texas, for about 10 days. On what I think was the 10th day, I was moved. A transportation officer asked those of us in the cell if we had eaten. And when some people said they hadn't, he told them to shut up.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>19. 我在德克萨斯州埃尔帕索被拘留了大约 10 天。我想大概是在第 10 天，我被转移了。一名押解官员问我们牢房里的人吃过东西没有。当有些人说没有时，他叫他们闭嘴。

00:53:14.030 --> 00:53:30.210
<v Marquis Rodriguez>20. I was moved to a detention center in Torrance, New Mexico. The bus ride to Torrance was scary. The bus swerved a lot because the driver was falling asleep. I thought we would die, for sure, and I wouldn't be able to do anything because I was chained.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>20. 我被转移到新墨西哥州托兰斯的拘留中心。去托兰斯的巴士之旅很吓人。巴士晃得很厉害，因为司机快睡着了。我确信我们死定了，而且因为我戴着镣铐什么也做不了。

00:53:30.210 --> 00:53:50.770
<v Marquis Rodriguez>After I arrived in Torrance, I had to wait for about 12 hours to be processed. They took our temperature, blood pressure, weight, and height, and I think gave us some type of vaccine against measles. Then I was moved to a room with about 50 other people. The food there was awful. It looked like dog food. I stayed at Torrance for six days.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>到达托兰斯后，我不得不等待大约 12 个小时来办理手续。他们测量了我们的体温、血压、体重和身高，我想还给我们打了某种麻疹疫苗。然后我被转移到一个有大约 50 个人的房间里。那里的食物糟透了。看起来像狗粮。我在托兰斯待了六天。

00:53:50.770 --> 00:54:16.290
<v Marquis Rodriguez>22. In Torrance, I was able to message and video call with my family, lawyer, and friends. I was only able to do this because teachers at my school had pooled money and put it in my account. You needed to pay to use the tablet. On Sunday the 25th, after dinner, I was moved to a single cell. It was small and cold. I couldn't sleep because it was so cold and because the guard had the TV on all night.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>22. 在托兰斯，我能够与家人、律师和朋友发信息和视频通话。我能做到这一点，完全是因为我学校的老师们凑钱把钱存进了我的账户。你需要付费才能使用平板电脑。25 日星期日晚饭后，我被转移到一个单人牢房。又小又冷。我无法入睡，因为太冷了，而且警卫一整夜都开着电视。

00:54:16.290 --> 00:54:48.760
<v Marquis Rodriguez>24. On Monday, January 26, I got breakfast around 4:30 AM. Around 10:00 AM, I was told we were going to Cibola jail. Nobody explained why. I was handcuffed and loaded into a car and taken to Cibola jail in New Mexico. 25. The Cibola jail was horrible. It was cramped. I was chained up again. The officers mocked me and called me their enemy. They laughed at me. I wasn't given an explanation for why I was there.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>24. 1 月 26 日星期一，我在清晨 4:30 左右吃了早饭。上午 10 点左右，我被告知我们要去西波拉监狱。没有人解释原因。我被戴上手铐塞进车里，带到了新墨西哥州的西波拉监狱。25. 西波拉监狱太可怕了。空间狭窄。我又被戴上了铁链。官员们嘲笑我，叫我敌人。他们嘲笑我。没有人向我解释我为什么会在那里。

00:54:48.760 --> 00:55:08.400
<v Marquis Rodriguez>26. Security guards then put me on a bus. I think it was around January 27. I was handcuffed. It was freezing cold in the bus because the driver had the AC on. We had no jackets. The bus eventually took me back to El Paso in the early morning of Tuesday, January 27.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>26. 然后保安把我塞进一辆巴士。我想大概是 1 月 27 日左右。我戴着手铐。巴士里冷得要命，因为司机开着空调。我们没有夹克。巴士最终在 1 月 27 日星期二清晨把我带回了埃尔帕索。

00:55:08.400 --> 00:55:46.910
<v Marquis Rodriguez>27. I was put in a cold cell where I had to sleep on the bare cement floor. One officer told me that I had no chance of returning to Minnesota and that the best thing for me is self-deportation. She told me that if I fought my case, I would spend two to three more months here in El Paso. She offered me $2,600 to self-deport. I refused. They didn't tell me the judge had already ordered my release and return to Minnesota. If I hadn't managed to talk to my attorney, who told me a while back that I was ordered released, I might have given up at this point and signed the self-deportation forms because the conditions were so unbearable.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>27. 我被关进了一个寒冷的牢房里，不得不睡在光秃秃的水泥地上。一名官员告诉我，我返回明尼苏达州的机会为零，对我和最好的办法就是自行遣返。她告诉我，如果我和我的案子抗争，我会在埃尔帕索多待两到三个月。她向我提供 2,600 美元让我自行遣返。我拒绝了。他们没有告诉我法官已经下令释放我并让我返回明尼苏达州。要是我没能设法联系到我的律师（她不久前曾告诉我法官下令释放我），由于条件实在无法忍受，我当时可能就已经放弃并签署自行遣返表格了。

00:55:46.910 --> 00:56:06.950
<v Marquis Rodriguez>28. I was taken to a doctor to check my temperature and blood pressure. I waited at the doctor's office until around 1:30 PM. And then suddenly, an ICE officer came and shouted my name. She told me I might be free, but don't get too excited. They took me to the airport.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>28. 我被带去见医生检查体温和血压。我在医务室一直等到下午 1:30 左右。接着突然间，一名移民及海关执法局官员走过来大声喊我的名字。她告诉我我可能会重获自由，但别太兴奋。他们把我带到了机场。

00:56:06.950 --> 00:56:12.550
<v Marquis Rodriguez>29. We arrived at the airport too late and missed the plane.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>29. 我们到达机场时太晚了，误了飞机。

00:56:12.550 --> 00:56:24.740
<v Marquis Rodriguez>30. When I was taken back to the detention center from the airport after missing the flight, I overheard an ICE officer saying in English, the judge in Minnesota wants this one back.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>30. 误机后我被从机场带回拘留中心时，我无意中听到一名移民及海关执法局官员用英语说，明尼苏达州的法官要把这个人弄回去。

00:56:24.740 --> 00:56:37.220
<v Marquis Rodriguez>31. At around midnight, they took me to another police processing station, 10 to 15 minutes away. At 5:00 AM, they took us to the airport. I was flown to Denver and then to Minneapolis.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>31. 午夜时分，他们把我带到另一个 10 到 15 分钟车程外的警察处理站。凌晨 5 点，他们把我们带到机场。我被用飞机送到丹佛，然后送到明尼阿波利斯。

00:56:37.220 --> 00:56:57.540
<v Marquis Rodriguez>32. When I arrived in Minneapolis, I was handed off to ICE agents, handcuffed, and put in jail at the same place where it all started with the same dirty floors. I asked for a phone call. I called my attorney but got no answer. I then called my teacher, and while I was speaking to her, I was told that I was being released.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>32. 当我抵达明尼阿波利斯时，我被移交给移民及海关执法局特工，戴上手铐，关进了最初一切开始的那个地板同样肮脏的监狱里。我要求打个电话。我给我的律师打电话，但没有人接。然后我给老师打电话，正跟她说话时，我被告知我要被释放了。

00:56:57.540 --> 00:57:10.700
<v Marquis Rodriguez>My teacher came to pick me up. I had to sign lots of papers saying that I wasn't going to be a part of any groups. I didn't know what they meant by groups. They never told me why I was detained.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>我的老师来接我。我不得不签署大量文件，声明我不会参与任何组织。我不知道他们所说的组织是什么意思。他们从来没有告诉我为什么要拘留我。

00:57:10.700 --> 00:57:40.260
<v Marquis Rodriguez>35. Right now, I am happy that I have been released. I am grateful I have my family and an attorney. When I think of my time in detention, I think of so many ways I could have died there, the way my car was stopped in the middle of the road, the diseases I could have caught at the Whipple, the driver who was falling asleep at the wheel, driving us from El Paso to New Mexico. I declare, under penalty of perjury, that the foregoing is true and correct.
<v Marquis Rodriguez>35. 现在，我很高兴我获释了。我很感激我有家人和律师。当我想起被拘留的时光时，我想到了我在那里丧命的无数种可能：我的车在路中间被拦下的方式，我在惠普尔可能染上的疾病，从埃尔帕索开往新墨西哥州时方向盘后打瞌睡的司机。我在此庄严宣誓，伪证罪不可恕，上述内容真实无误。

00:57:40.260 --> 00:57:52.490
<v David Kestenbaum>In the end, Oscar was detained for 18 days.
<v David Kestenbaum>最后，奥斯卡被拘留了 18 天。

00:57:52.490 --> 00:58:44.470
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum is the senior editor of our show. Julie Le, the ICE attorney in the transcript, has since been reassigned. The declaration of O was read by Marquis Rodriguez. Rich Sommer read the court transcript. We reached out to the Department of Justice for comment and got the following statement that reads in part, "If rogue judges followed the law in adjudicating cases and respected the government's obligation to properly prepare cases, there wouldn't be an overwhelming habeas caseload or concern over DHS following orders."
<v Ira Glass>大卫·克斯滕鲍姆是我们节目的资深编辑。文稿中提到的移民和海关执法局律师朱莉·勒此后已被重新分配工作。O 的声明由马奎斯·罗德里格斯宣读。里奇·萨默宣读了法庭记录。我们联系了司法部寻求置评，并得到了以下声明，其中部分内容写道：“如果流氓法官在裁决案件时遵守法律，并尊重政府妥善准备案件的义务，就不会出现海湾人身保护令案件压积的情况，也不会对国土安全部执行命令产生担忧。”

00:58:44.470 --> 00:59:09.590
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Lilly Sullivan and edited by Nancy Updike. The people who put together today's program include Phia Bennin, Dana Chivvis, Michal Comite, Aviva DeKornfeld, Cassie Howley, Seth Lind, Molly Marcello, Katherine Rae Mondo, Stowe Nelson, Anthony Roman, Ryan Rumery, Alissa Shipp, Ike Sriskandarajah, Christopher Swetala, and Diane Wu. Our managing editor is Sarah Abdurrahman. Our executive editor is Emanuele Berry. Special thanks today to Jeremy deVine and Suzanne Gaber.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由莉莉·沙利文制作，南希·厄普代克编辑。参与制作今天节目的还有菲亚·贝宁、达纳·奇维斯、米歇尔·科米特、阿维瓦·德科恩费尔德、卡西·豪利、塞思·林德、莫利·马塞洛、凯瑟琳·雷·蒙多、斯托·纳尔逊、安东尼·罗曼、瑞安·鲁梅里、阿利萨·希普、艾克·斯里斯坎达拉贾、克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉和黛安·吴。我们的常务编辑是萨拉·阿卜杜拉曼。我们的执行主编是埃马努埃莱·贝里。今天特别感谢杰里米·德维恩和苏珊娜·加贝尔。

00:59:09.590 --> 00:59:34.180
<v Ira Glass>Our website, where you can listen to our entire archive of over 800 episodes for absolutely free, thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia. He was visiting the Castro recently, went to a bar filled with big, hairy guys, took one look around and said--
